const lessonDocs = {"p1-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Unsorted values; return whether any number appears twice.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Sorted numbers; find a pair with a target sum using constant extra memory.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>An array is a row of boxes. A hash set is a super-fast “have I seen this?” notebook. A hash map is the same notebook but each key can store extra information such as a count, an index, or a group.</p><p>Imagine checking guests at a party. Re-scanning the whole guest list for every new person is slow. A set is the bouncer’s stamped list: one quick lookup tells you whether a name has already appeared.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Trade a little extra memory for fast lookup. While scanning the input once, remember exactly the information future elements will need.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>For Two Sum on [2,7,11,15] with target 9: start with an empty map. At 2, the needed complement is 7, not stored, so remember 2. At 7, the needed complement is 2, which is already stored. Stop. We replaced “try every pair” with “remember what I have seen.”</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> For each element, scan the rest of the array to find a duplicate, partner, or matching property. That repeated inner scan is usually O(n²).</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Ask what the inner loop is trying to discover. If it is only asking “have I seen X?”, “how many X?”, or “where did X occur?”, store that answer in a set/map once and reuse it in O(1) average time.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>A single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Arrays & Hashing to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 599}, "p1-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. Build a picture you can reconstruct instead of memorizing code.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Prefer structural clues over keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>“duplicate”, “seen before”, “unique”, or “frequency/count”</li><li>Need a complement such as target - current</li><li>Need to group items by a stable key such as a sorted word or character-count signature</li><li>Need O(1)-ish membership while scanning</li><li>Need prefix/suffix information without recomputing a whole range</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>The input is an array</li><li>The answer asks for an index</li><li>The problem says “find” or “count” without saying what relationship matters</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>The input is sorted and two endpoints can eliminate candidates — Two Pointers may use O(1) extra memory</li><li>The problem is about a contiguous range that grows/shrinks — Sliding Window is often the deeper structure</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: A single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Ask what the inner loop is trying to discover. If it is only asking “have I seen X?”, “how many X?”, or “where did X occur?”, store that answer in a set/map once and reuse it in O(1) average time.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Trade a little extra memory for fast lookup. While scanning the input once, remember exactly the information future elements will need.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Sorted array + “find two values summing to target” can be solved with Two Pointers in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. “Array + pair” alone does not force hashing.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: “duplicate”, “seen before”, “unique”, or “frequency/count”, Need a complement such as target - current?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “After processing index i, the set/map contains exactly the information from the processed prefix that later elements are allowed to query.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 595}, "p1-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>For each element, scan the rest of the array to find a duplicate, partner, or matching property. That repeated inner scan is usually O(n²).</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Ask what the inner loop is trying to discover. If it is only asking “have I seen X?”, “how many X?”, or “where did X occur?”, store that answer in a set/map once and reuse it in O(1) average time.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>After processing index i, the set/map contains exactly the information from the processed prefix that later elements are allowed to query.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">seen = {}   # set or map\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    # query information already remembered\n    if needed_key(x) in seen:\n        ...\n    # update memory for future elements\n    seen[x] = i</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>A single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty input</li><li>one element</li><li>many duplicates</li><li>negative numbers</li><li>zero</li><li>hash key must be immutable/canonical</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Insert before checking and accidentally reuse the same element</li><li>Use a map when a set is enough</li><li>Sort for an anagram key without noticing a cheaper fixed-size frequency signature</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 551}, "p1-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Contains Duplicate: membership only → set</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Two Sum: complement lookup → value→index map</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Group Anagrams: canonical signature → list of words</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Longest Consecutive: set + only start sequences at values whose predecessor is absent</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Can values repeat?</li><li>Do I return values or original indices?</li><li>May I sort the input, or must order be preserved?</li><li>Is extra O(n) memory acceptable?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Insert before checking and accidentally reuse the same element</li><li>Use a map when a set is enough</li><li>Sort for an anagram key without noticing a cheaper fixed-size frequency signature</li><li>Assume hash maps are ordered or worst-case O(1)</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Trade a little extra memory for fast lookup. While scanning the input once, remember exactly the information future elements will need.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 517}, "p1-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Two Pointers</b></td><td>sorted pair/triple relationship and pointer movement can eliminate candidates</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sliding Window</b></td><td>the state describes one contiguous moving range</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prefix Sum</b></td><td>many range-sum queries need a reusable cumulative total rather than membership</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>Sorted array + “find two values summing to target” can be solved with Two Pointers in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. “Array + pair” alone does not force hashing.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Contains Duplicate: membership only → set</li><li>Two Sum: complement lookup → value→index map</li><li>Group Anagrams: canonical signature → list of words</li><li>Longest Consecutive: set + only start sequences at values whose predecessor is absent</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>“duplicate”, “seen before”, “unique”, or “frequency/count”</li><li>Need a complement such as target - current</li><li>Need to group items by a stable key such as a sorted word or character-count signature</li><li>Need O(1)-ish membership while scanning</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>The input is an array</li><li>The answer asks for an index</li><li>The problem says “find” or “count” without saying what relationship matters</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Sorted array + “find two values summing to target” can be solved with Two Pointers in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. “Array + pair” alone does not force hashing.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Unsorted values; return whether any number appears twice.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Words must be grouped when they contain the same letters with the same counts.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Sorted numbers; find a pair with a target sum using constant extra memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find the longest run of consecutive integers even though the input is unsorted.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 571}, "p1-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard hashing questions usually hide the key. The breakthrough is often inventing the right representation: a frequency vector, prefix state, normalized signature, or boundary rule that turns a messy object into something hashable.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Prefix/suffix products let each position know “everything except me” without division</li><li>Bucket counting can replace sorting when the count range is bounded</li><li>Sequence-start detection prevents repeatedly walking the same consecutive run</li><li>XOR can replace a set only in special cancellation/parity problems</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Whenever you see an O(n²) inner loop that only searches for a value, ask “Can a map answer this instantly?”</li><li>Write the key and value meaning before coding: key = ?, value = ?</li><li>If you only need existence, prefer a set; fewer moving parts means fewer bugs</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>After processing index i, the set/map contains exactly the information from the processed prefix that later elements are allowed to query.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>A single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 547}, "p1-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Arrays & Hashing\n\nCORE → Trade a little extra memory for fast lookup. While scanning the input once, remember exactly the information future elements will need.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- “duplicate”, “seen before”, “unique”, or “frequency/count”\n- Need a complement such as target - current\n- Need to group items by a stable key such as a sorted word or character-count signature\n\nINVARIANT\nAfter processing index i, the set/map contains exactly the information from the processed prefix that later elements are allowed to query.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nA single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nHASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Unsorted values; return whether any number appears twice.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Words must be grouped when they contain the same letters with the same counts.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Sorted numbers; find a pair with a target sum using constant extra memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find the longest run of consecutive integers even though the input is unsorted.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: A single pass is O(n). Average hash lookup/update is O(1), so n operations × O(1) = O(n). The map/set may hold up to n items, so auxiliary space is O(n).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">seen = {}   # set or map\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    # query information already remembered\n    if needed_key(x) in seen:\n        ...\n    # update memory for future elements\n    seen[x] = i</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Arrays & Hashing come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">array: [ 2 ][ 7 ][11 ][15 ]\n          ↓\nseen:   {2:index0}\n          ↓ next x=7, need 9-7=2 ✓\n\nONE PASS + MEMORY  →  avoid repeated rescans</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">BRUTE FORCE\nitem → scan everything again → scan again → scan again\n                 O(n²)\n                    ↓ remember answers\nHASHING\nitem → O(1) lookup/update → next item\n                 O(n) average</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HASHING = remember useful facts from the past so the future does not rescan it.</p></section>", "wordCount": 583}, "p2-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted numbers; find two values with exact target sum.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>Two pointers means you keep two positions instead of trying every possible pair. The positions may start at opposite ends, move together in one direction, or move at different speeds.</p><p>Imagine searching a sorted shelf for two books whose prices total $20. If the cheapest + most expensive is too large, choosing an even more expensive book cannot help; move the expensive side inward. One comparison eliminates many pairs.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Move a pointer only when you can prove that every candidate you just skipped cannot produce a better valid answer.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Sorted [1,2,4,7,9], target 11. L=1,R=9 gives 10, too small, so L must increase. Now 2+9=11. We never test 1 with 7,4,2 because if 1+9 was already too small, pairing 1 with anything smaller cannot reach 11.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Try every i with every j. For pair problems that is O(n²) candidate pairs.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Ordering or a monotonic relationship tells you which side is hopeless. A pointer movement does not just move one step; logically it discards an entire family of pairs.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>If left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Two Pointers to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 564}, "p2-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Sorted array + pair/triple target</li><li>Palindrome or symmetric comparison from both ends</li><li>In-place remove/partition/compact while preserving a processed region</li><li>Two boundaries define an objective such as area</li><li>Fast/slow motion suggests cycle, middle, or “k behind” logic</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>There are two variables in the problem</li><li>The input is an array</li><li>The word “pair” appears even though data is unsorted and no ordering can eliminate candidates</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Need membership/complement on unsorted input → Hash Map may be cleaner</li><li>Need to maintain properties of every element inside a contiguous range → Sliding Window</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: If left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Ordering or a monotonic relationship tells you which side is hopeless. A pointer movement does not just move one step; logically it discards an entire family of pairs.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Move a pointer only when you can prove that every candidate you just skipped cannot produce a better valid answer.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Longest substring with at most 2 distinct characters” uses two indices, but the important state is the entire substring and its frequency counts. That is Sliding Window, not generic Two Pointers.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Sorted array + pair/triple target, Palindrome or symmetric comparison from both ends?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “Everything outside the active pointer region has already been proved impossible, finalized, or placed correctly.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 595}, "p2-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Try every i with every j. For pair problems that is O(n²) candidate pairs.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Ordering or a monotonic relationship tells you which side is hopeless. A pointer movement does not just move one step; logically it discards an entire family of pairs.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>Everything outside the active pointer region has already been proved impossible, finalized, or placed correctly.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">left, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    state = evaluate(nums[left], nums[right])\n    if state == target:\n        return ...\n    elif state_too_small(state):\n        left += 1\n    else:\n        right -= 1</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>If left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>duplicate values</li><li>pointers crossing</li><li>one/two element inputs</li><li>negative values</li><li>sorting changes original positions</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Move both sides “because it feels right” without proof</li><li>Forget duplicate skipping in 3Sum</li><li>Use <= instead of < and reuse the same element</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 500}, "p2-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Pair sum in sorted input</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>3Sum = sort + freeze one value + solve a 2-pointer subproblem</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Container With Most Water = endpoints define objective; move limiting wall</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Trapping Rain Water = boundary knowledge and smaller-side finalization</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Fast/slow linked-list cycle detection = relative speed rather than opposite ends</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Is the input sorted?</li><li>May I sort, and do I need original indices?</li><li>Can duplicates appear?</li><li>Do I need one answer or all unique answers?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Move both sides “because it feels right” without proof</li><li>Forget duplicate skipping in 3Sum</li><li>Use <= instead of < and reuse the same element</li><li>Call every left/right loop a sliding window</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Move a pointer only when you can prove that every candidate you just skipped cannot produce a better valid answer.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 519}, "p2-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Sliding Window</b></td><td>left/right describe a whole contiguous window with validity state</td></tr><tr><td><b>Binary Search</b></td><td>one midpoint halves a monotonic search space</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hash Map</b></td><td>unsorted complement lookup is easier with remembered values</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Longest substring with at most 2 distinct characters” uses two indices, but the important state is the entire substring and its frequency counts. That is Sliding Window, not generic Two Pointers.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Pair sum in sorted input</li><li>3Sum = sort + freeze one value + solve a 2-pointer subproblem</li><li>Container With Most Water = endpoints define objective; move limiting wall</li><li>Trapping Rain Water = boundary knowledge and smaller-side finalization</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Sorted array + pair/triple target</li><li>Palindrome or symmetric comparison from both ends</li><li>In-place remove/partition/compact while preserving a processed region</li><li>Two boundaries define an objective such as area</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>There are two variables in the problem</li><li>The input is an array</li><li>The word “pair” appears even though data is unsorted and no ordering can eliminate candidates</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Longest substring with at most 2 distinct characters” uses two indices, but the important state is the entire substring and its frequency counts. That is Sliding Window, not generic Two Pointers.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted numbers; find two values with exact target sum.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>String; determine if it reads the same forward and backward while ignoring punctuation.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Heights; choose two endpoints to maximize contained area.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 560}, "p2-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard versions remove the obvious “sum” clue. Look for a boundary that limits the answer. If changing the non-limiting boundary can never help, that is the proof that tells you which pointer moves.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>The “limiting side” idea powers Container With Most Water</li><li>Trapping Rain Water finalizes the side with the smaller known maximum</li><li>Fast/slow pointers work because relative speed eventually creates a collision in a cycle</li><li>Partition scans are another two-pointer family</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Say out loud: “What candidates disappear if I move left? What disappears if I move right?”</li><li>If you cannot justify either move, this may not be a two-pointer problem</li><li>On sorted input, compare current state to target and let the sign/direction choose the move</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>Everything outside the active pointer region has already been proved impossible, finalized, or placed correctly.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>If left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 549}, "p2-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Two Pointers\n\nCORE → Move a pointer only when you can prove that every candidate you just skipped cannot produce a better valid answer.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Sorted array + pair/triple target\n- Palindrome or symmetric comparison from both ends\n- In-place remove/partition/compact while preserving a processed region\n\nINVARIANT\nEverything outside the active pointer region has already been proved impossible, finalized, or placed correctly.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nIf left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nTWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted numbers; find two values with exact target sum.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>String; determine if it reads the same forward and backward while ignoring punctuation.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Heights; choose two endpoints to maximize contained area.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: If left only moves right and right only moves left, each pointer moves at most n times. Total moves ≤ 2n, which simplifies to O(n). With only pointer variables, extra space is O(1). Sorting first changes total time to O(n log n).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">left, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    state = evaluate(nums[left], nums[right])\n    if state == target:\n        return ...\n    elif state_too_small(state):\n        left += 1\n    else:\n        right -= 1</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Two Pointers come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">L → [1][2][4][7][9] ← R\n      1 + 9 = 10 too small\n      discard every pair using 1 with a smaller right value\n          L → [2][4][7][9] ← R</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">QUESTION\nCan one boundary move eliminate MANY candidates?\n          │\n        yes\n          ↓\nCan you PROVE which side is safe to move?\n          │\n        yes → TWO POINTERS</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TWO POINTERS = move boundaries only when the move safely kills a whole group of candidates.</p></section>", "wordCount": 556}, "p3-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find two sorted values whose sum is target.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A sliding window is a moving contiguous slice of an array or string. Instead of recomputing everything inside every possible slice, keep a small summary and update it when the right side enters or the left side leaves.</p><p>Think of a camera frame moving across a parade. You do not recount every person in the city each time the frame moves; you add the person entering the frame and remove the person leaving it.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Right expands the candidate range. Left repairs validity or preserves a fixed size. The state must describe exactly what is inside the current contiguous window.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>For “longest substring without repeating characters,” R adds each character to a set/count map. If a duplicate appears, move L and remove characters until the window is unique again. Update the best length only when the window is valid.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Enumerate every starting point, every ending point, and often rescan the range. That can reach O(n²) or O(n³).</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Reuse the previous range. When R advances, add one element. When L advances, subtract/remove one element. If both pointers only move forward, total pointer motion is O(n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>The loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Sliding Window to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 579}, "p3-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>“subarray” or “substring” plus longest/shortest/best/count</li><li>Contiguous range with at most/at least/exactly K property</li><li>Need a fixed-size range statistic</li><li>Validity changes as elements enter and leave</li><li>Each element should be added and removed only a small number of times</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any problem with left/right indices</li><li>Any “longest” problem</li><li>Any array optimization even if selected items are non-contiguous</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Need pair relationship between endpoints rather than state of whole range → Two Pointers</li><li>Negative numbers make a sum condition non-monotonic; Prefix Sum/map may be needed</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: The loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Reuse the previous range. When R advances, add one element. When L advances, subtract/remove one element. If both pointers only move forward, total pointer motion is O(n).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Right expands the candidate range. Left repairs validity or preserves a fixed size. The state must describe exactly what is inside the current contiguous window.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Maximum sum increasing subsequence” mentions a sequence and maximum, but the chosen items are not required to be contiguous. Sliding Window does not fit; DP is a better candidate.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: “subarray” or “substring” plus longest/shortest/best/count, Contiguous range with at most/at least/exactly K property?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The maintained state exactly describes data[left:right+1], and after the repair loop the required window condition is valid.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 589}, "p3-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Enumerate every starting point, every ending point, and often rescan the range. That can reach O(n²) or O(n³).</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Reuse the previous range. When R advances, add one element. When L advances, subtract/remove one element. If both pointers only move forward, total pointer motion is O(n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The maintained state exactly describes data[left:right+1], and after the repair loop the required window condition is valid.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">left = 0\nstate = ...\nfor right, x in enumerate(data):\n    add(x, state)\n    while not valid(state):\n        remove(data[left], state)\n        left += 1\n    answer = update(answer, left, right, state)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>The loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty string</li><li>window of size 0/1</li><li>all same characters</li><li>all unique</li><li>condition never valid</li><li>condition always valid</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Update answer before repairing when only valid windows count</li><li>Forget to decrement/remove state when left moves</li><li>Use a while loop when a fixed window only needs one removal</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 540}, "p3-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Fixed-size: max sum of every k-length subarray</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Variable-size: longest valid substring</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Minimum window: shrink while still valid</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Frequency matching: permutation/anagram windows</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Monotonic deque: keep max/min candidates inside each window</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Must chosen elements be contiguous?</li><li>Are values non-negative if the condition uses sums?</li><li>Is the goal longest, shortest, exact count, or number of valid windows?</li><li>Does removing the left item let me update state cheaply?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Update answer before repairing when only valid windows count</li><li>Forget to decrement/remove state when left moves</li><li>Use a while loop when a fixed window only needs one removal</li><li>Assume sum-based shrink logic works with negatives</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Right expands the candidate range. Left repairs validity or preserves a fixed size. The state must describe exactly what is inside the current contiguous window.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 530}, "p3-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Two Pointers</b></td><td>endpoints are the candidates; interior state is not the main object</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prefix Sum</b></td><td>range query is answered algebraically instead of incrementally maintaining validity</td></tr><tr><td><b>Binary Search</b></td><td>you search a monotonic answer size and call a window feasibility check</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Maximum sum increasing subsequence” mentions a sequence and maximum, but the chosen items are not required to be contiguous. Sliding Window does not fit; DP is a better candidate.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Fixed-size: max sum of every k-length subarray</li><li>Variable-size: longest valid substring</li><li>Minimum window: shrink while still valid</li><li>Frequency matching: permutation/anagram windows</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>“subarray” or “substring” plus longest/shortest/best/count</li><li>Contiguous range with at most/at least/exactly K property</li><li>Need a fixed-size range statistic</li><li>Validity changes as elements enter and leave</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any problem with left/right indices</li><li>Any “longest” problem</li><li>Any array optimization even if selected items are non-contiguous</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Maximum sum increasing subsequence” mentions a sequence and maximum, but the chosen items are not required to be contiguous. Sliding Window does not fit; DP is a better candidate.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Maximum sum of any exactly-k consecutive values.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find two sorted values whose sum is target.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Smallest substring containing all required character counts.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 528}, "p3-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard sliding-window questions usually make validity non-obvious. Find a summary that can be updated when one item enters/leaves. Once validity becomes O(1) to test, the rest often collapses to the standard expand/repair skeleton.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>At-most-K counting can be combined to get exactly-K</li><li>A stale maximum frequency can still be safe in Character Replacement</li><li>Monotonic deques drop values that can never become future maxima</li><li>Minimum Window uses “formed vs required” rather than comparing full maps each time</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write “WINDOW STATE = ____” before code</li><li>Write the exact invalid condition as one Boolean expression</li><li>If both pointers only move forward, do not fear the apparent nested while loop</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The maintained state exactly describes data[left:right+1], and after the repair loop the required window condition is valid.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>The loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 540}, "p3-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Sliding Window\n\nCORE → Right expands the candidate range. Left repairs validity or preserves a fixed size. The state must describe exactly what is inside the current contiguous window.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- “subarray” or “substring” plus longest/shortest/best/count\n- Contiguous range with at most/at least/exactly K property\n- Need a fixed-size range statistic\n\nINVARIANT\nThe maintained state exactly describes data[left:right+1], and after the repair loop the required window condition is valid.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nThe loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nSLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest substring with no repeated characters.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Maximum sum of any exactly-k consecutive values.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find two sorted values whose sum is target.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Smallest substring containing all required character counts.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: The loop looks nested, but left never moves backward. Right moves n times and left moves at most n times, so total work is about 2n plus state updates: O(n). Space depends on window state: O(1) for sums, O(k) or O(alphabet) for counts.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">left = 0\nstate = ...\nfor right, x in enumerate(data):\n    add(x, state)\n    while not valid(state):\n        remove(data[left], state)\n        left += 1\n    answer = update(answer, left, right, state)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Sliding Window come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[ a ][ b ][ c ][ a ][ d ]\n  L →          ← R\nwindow = \"abca\"  invalid (a twice)\nremove left a\n       L → [b][c][a] ← R  valid again</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">R EXPANDS → learn more\nL REPAIRS → restore rule\nSTATE      → describes exactly the active range\nANSWER     → updated at the correct moment</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> SLIDING WINDOW = contiguous range + cheap state updates + right explores, left repairs.</p></section>", "wordCount": 571}, "p4-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Check whether brackets are correctly nested.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Continuously return the kth largest number.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A stack is a pile where you can only use the top item first: last in, first out. A monotonic stack is a stack whose stored values stay increasing or decreasing so a new value can resolve old unfinished values.</p><p>A stack is like plates: the last plate placed on top is the first plate removed. A monotonic stack is like keeping only “still possible champions”; a stronger new value knocks out weaker candidates that can never matter again.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Store unresolved work. Pop exactly when the current input provides the missing information needed to finish the item on top.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Daily Temperatures: store indices whose warmer day is not known yet. At temperature 75, if the stack top is day 2 with 71, pop day 2 and record the distance. Keep popping while 75 is warmer than the top.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> For every index, scan right until finding the next greater/smaller value. That can be O(n²).</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Keep unresolved indices in order. When a new value is strong enough to resolve the top, pop it. An item is pushed once and popped once, so the repeated-looking while loop is amortized O(n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Stack & Monotonic Stack to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 588}, "p4-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Matching nested brackets/tags</li><li>Undo, backtracking history, or “most recent unresolved” state</li><li>Expression/postfix evaluation</li><li>Next/previous greater or smaller element</li><li>A future value resolves several earlier positions</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Need any collection of prior values</li><li>Need the largest value globally — that is usually a heap or simple maximum</li><li>A recursive problem merely uses the call stack internally</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Repeatedly need globally best candidate → Heap</li><li>Need a moving contiguous range → Sliding Window</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Keep unresolved indices in order. When a new value is strong enough to resolve the top, pop it. An item is pushed once and popped once, so the repeated-looking while loop is amortized O(n).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Store unresolved work. Pop exactly when the current input provides the missing information needed to finish the item on top.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find the kth largest number seen so far” needs the best among many candidates, not last-in-first-out resolution. Use a heap, not a stack.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Matching nested brackets/tags, Undo, backtracking history, or “most recent unresolved” state?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “Every entry remaining on the stack is unresolved, and the stack order preserves the monotonic/nesting property required for future resolution.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 587}, "p4-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>For every index, scan right until finding the next greater/smaller value. That can be O(n²).</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Keep unresolved indices in order. When a new value is strong enough to resolve the top, pop it. An item is pushed once and popped once, so the repeated-looking while loop is amortized O(n).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>Every entry remaining on the stack is unresolved, and the stack order preserves the monotonic/nesting property required for future resolution.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">stack = []\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    while stack and x > nums[stack[-1]]:\n        j = stack.pop()\n        answer[j] = i - j\n    stack.append(i)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>duplicates</li><li>strict vs non-strict comparison</li><li>nothing ever pops</li><li>everything pops immediately</li><li>flush remaining items at end</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Store values when the answer needs indices</li><li>Use > instead of >= (or vice versa) without deciding duplicate semantics</li><li>Pop before calculating the result that pop represents</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 546}, "p4-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Valid Parentheses: plain nested LIFO</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Min Stack: stack + auxiliary min state</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>RPN evaluation: operands wait for operator</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Daily Temperatures: next greater element</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Largest Rectangle: nearest smaller boundaries</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Car Fleet: monotonic arrival/fleet merging</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Do I need nearest greater/smaller or global greatest?</li><li>How should equal values behave?</li><li>Should stack store values, indices, or pairs?</li><li>When exactly is an item considered resolved?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Store values when the answer needs indices</li><li>Use > instead of >= (or vice versa) without deciding duplicate semantics</li><li>Pop before calculating the result that pop represents</li><li>Forget operand order in subtraction/division</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Store unresolved work. Pop exactly when the current input provides the missing information needed to finish the item on top.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 526}, "p4-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Heap</b></td><td>best among all candidates, not most recently unresolved</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sliding Window</b></td><td>active contiguous region rather than unresolved history</td></tr><tr><td><b>Recursion</b></td><td>implicit call stack may replace an explicit stack for tree/DFS structure</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Find the kth largest number seen so far” needs the best among many candidates, not last-in-first-out resolution. Use a heap, not a stack.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Valid Parentheses: plain nested LIFO</li><li>Min Stack: stack + auxiliary min state</li><li>RPN evaluation: operands wait for operator</li><li>Daily Temperatures: next greater element</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Matching nested brackets/tags</li><li>Undo, backtracking history, or “most recent unresolved” state</li><li>Expression/postfix evaluation</li><li>Next/previous greater or smaller element</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Need any collection of prior values</li><li>Need the largest value globally — that is usually a heap or simple maximum</li><li>A recursive problem merely uses the call stack internally</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find the kth largest number seen so far” needs the best among many candidates, not last-in-first-out resolution. Use a heap, not a stack.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Check whether brackets are correctly nested.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>For every day, find how many days until a warmer temperature.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Continuously return the kth largest number.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find the largest rectangle formed by histogram bars.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 527}, "p4-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard monotonic-stack problems hide the boundary interpretation. Ask what would be the first thing to the left/right that invalidates or dominates the current item. Once those boundaries are known, area/range formulas become straightforward.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Sentinels can force all pending items to resolve</li><li>Histogram width emerges from previous/next smaller boundaries</li><li>Monotonic stacks compress nested scans into amortized linear work</li><li>A stack can store richer state such as (value,index,count)</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Before coding, finish this sentence: “An item stays on my stack while ____; it pops when ____.”</li><li>Draw the stack vertically for one example</li><li>If you cannot say whether duplicates pop, your invariant is incomplete</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>Every entry remaining on the stack is unresolved, and the stack order preserves the monotonic/nesting property required for future resolution.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 544}, "p4-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Stack & Monotonic Stack\n\nCORE → Store unresolved work. Pop exactly when the current input provides the missing information needed to finish the item on top.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Matching nested brackets/tags\n- Undo, backtracking history, or “most recent unresolved” state\n- Expression/postfix evaluation\n\nINVARIANT\nEvery entry remaining on the stack is unresolved, and the stack order preserves the monotonic/nesting property required for future resolution.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nEach index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nSTACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Check whether brackets are correctly nested.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>For every day, find how many days until a warmer temperature.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Continuously return the kth largest number.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find the largest rectangle formed by histogram bars.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Each index is pushed once and popped at most once. That is at most 2n stack operations: O(n) time. The stack can hold up to n indices, so O(n) space. Plain bracket stacks are also O(n) worst-case space.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">stack = []\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    while stack and x > nums[stack[-1]]:\n        j = stack.pop()\n        answer[j] = i - j\n    stack.append(i)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Stack & Monotonic Stack come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">new = 75\nstack temperatures bottom→top: [60, 68, 71]\n                                   ↑ pop 71 (resolved)\n                              ↑ pop 68 (resolved)\nthen push 75 → [60, 75]</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PUSH = “I do not know your answer yet.”\nPOP  = “The current item finally proves your answer.”\n\nmonotonic order = remove candidates that can never matter again</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> STACK = unresolved work; MONOTONIC STACK = unresolved work kept in an order that lets new items resolve old ones fast.</p></section>", "wordCount": 574}, "p5-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted array; find target index.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Unsorted values; find whether a complement exists.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>Binary search repeatedly asks one yes/no comparison that lets you throw away half of the remaining possibilities. It works on sorted data and on any answer space where feasibility changes in one direction only.</p><p>Guess a number from 1 to 100. Asking “is it above 50?” is powerful because one answer kills 50 possibilities. Then ask around 75, then 62, and so on.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Find a monotonic boundary and maintain an interval that is guaranteed to still contain the answer.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Find 7 in [1,3,5,7,9,11,13]. Mid is 7 immediately. If target were 11, 7 is too small, so everything left of and including 7 is impossible; continue only in the right half.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Scan every candidate from left to right, or try every possible numeric answer. That is O(n) or worse.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>After k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Binary Search to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section>", "wordCount": 553}, "p5-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Sorted input</li><li>Find first/last occurrence or insertion boundary</li><li>“minimum possible maximum” / “maximum possible minimum”</li><li>A yes/no feasibility test changes only once as candidate answer grows</li><li>Rotated sorted array where one half remains ordered</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>The answer is a number</li><li>The array has indices</li><li>The problem says “search” but no ordering/monotonicity exists</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Need interacting pair of endpoints → Two Pointers</li><li>Need arbitrary lookup in unsorted values → Hash Map</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: After k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Find a monotonic boundary and maintain an interval that is guaranteed to still contain the answer.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find two numbers in a sorted array summing to target” is sorted, but Two Pointers uses the pair relationship directly and achieves O(n); binary-searching the partner for every item is O(n log n).</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Sorted input, Find first/last occurrence or insertion boundary?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The true answer, if it exists, always remains inside the current [lo, hi] candidate range.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section>", "wordCount": 584}, "p5-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Scan every candidate from left to right, or try every possible numeric answer. That is O(n) or worse.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The true answer, if it exists, always remains inside the current [lo, hi] candidate range.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\nwhile lo <= hi:\n    mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n    if is_answer(mid):\n        return mid\n    elif answer_is_right(mid):\n        lo = mid + 1\n    else:\n        hi = mid - 1</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>After k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty array</li><li>one element</li><li>target below/above all values</li><li>duplicates</li><li>integer midpoint/overflow</li><li>closed vs half-open interval</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Mix lo<=hi with a half-open update rule</li><li>Move lo=mid instead of mid+1 and get stuck</li><li>Reverse predicate direction</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Scan every candidate from left to right, or try every possible numeric answer. That is O(n) or worse. The useful idea is: If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p></section>", "wordCount": 529}, "p5-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Exact element lookup</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Lower/upper bound</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>First true / last false boundary</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Rotated sorted array</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Koko Eating Bananas: binary search speed, not array index</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Median of Two Sorted Arrays: binary search a partition</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Is input sorted ascending?</li><li>Can duplicates occur, and do I need first/last?</li><li>What are the minimum and maximum possible answers?</li><li>Is the feasibility predicate monotonic?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Mix lo<=hi with a half-open update rule</li><li>Move lo=mid instead of mid+1 and get stuck</li><li>Reverse predicate direction</li><li>Binary-search an answer whose feasibility is not monotonic</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Find a monotonic boundary and maintain an interval that is guaranteed to still contain the answer.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Scan every candidate from left to right, or try every possible numeric answer. That is O(n) or worse. The useful idea is: If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p></section>", "wordCount": 510}, "p5-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Two Pointers</b></td><td>two candidates interact and both may move</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hash Map</b></td><td>unsorted exact membership can be O(1) average</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greedy</b></td><td>one pass maintains a best frontier rather than halving possibilities</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Find two numbers in a sorted array summing to target” is sorted, but Two Pointers uses the pair relationship directly and achieves O(n); binary-searching the partner for every item is O(n log n).</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Exact element lookup</li><li>Lower/upper bound</li><li>First true / last false boundary</li><li>Rotated sorted array</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Sorted input</li><li>Find first/last occurrence or insertion boundary</li><li>“minimum possible maximum” / “maximum possible minimum”</li><li>A yes/no feasibility test changes only once as candidate answer grows</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>The answer is a number</li><li>The array has indices</li><li>The problem says “search” but no ordering/monotonicity exists</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find two numbers in a sorted array summing to target” is sorted, but Two Pointers uses the pair relationship directly and achieves O(n); binary-searching the partner for every item is O(n log n).</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted array; find target index.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find the minimum eating speed that finishes all piles by H hours.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Unsorted values; find whether a complement exists.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find first index where a monotonic predicate becomes true.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Scan every candidate from left to right, or try every possible numeric answer. That is O(n) or worse. The useful idea is: If one comparison tells you an entire half cannot contain the answer, you can shrink n → n/2 → n/4 → … until one candidate remains.</p></section>", "wordCount": 556}, "p5-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard binary-search problems hide the search space. The key question is not “is the input sorted?” but “Can I order possible answers so that once one answer works, every larger/smaller answer also works?”</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Binary search is really boundary search, not just “find item in sorted list”</li><li>Rotated arrays still contain an ordered half each step</li><li>Answer-space binary search is common in capacity/speed/allocation problems</li><li>Partition binary search can solve medians without merging arrays</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write the predicate in English before code: “candidate x is feasible if…”</li><li>Draw F F F T T T or T T T F F F</li><li>Use one trusted interval convention for months until it becomes automatic</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The true answer, if it exists, always remains inside the current [lo, hi] candidate range.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>After k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section>", "wordCount": 509}, "p5-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Binary Search\n\nCORE → Find a monotonic boundary and maintain an interval that is guaranteed to still contain the answer.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Sorted input\n- Find first/last occurrence or insertion boundary\n- “minimum possible maximum” / “maximum possible minimum”\n\nINVARIANT\nThe true answer, if it exists, always remains inside the current [lo, hi] candidate range.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nAfter k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nBINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Sorted array; find target index.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find the minimum eating speed that finishes all piles by H hours.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Unsorted values; find whether a complement exists.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find first index where a monotonic predicate becomes true.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: After k checks the search space is n/2^k. Stop when that becomes about 1: 2^k ≈ n, so k ≈ log₂n. Therefore O(log n) checks. If each feasibility check costs O(n), binary search on answer becomes O(n log range).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\nwhile lo <= hi:\n    mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2\n    if is_answer(mid):\n        return mid\n    elif answer_is_right(mid):\n        lo = mid + 1\n    else:\n        hi = mid - 1</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Binary Search come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">[1 3 5 7 9 11 13]\n       M\n       7 < target 11\nX X X X [9 11 13]\n          M\n          11 ✓</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">candidate answers: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\nfeasible?          F F F F T T T T\n                           ↑\n                    find FIRST TRUE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BINARY SEARCH = monotonic decision + throw away half with proof.</p></section>", "wordCount": 504}, "p6-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Reverse a singly linked chain in place.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find a value in a sorted array by halving the search space.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A linked list is a chain of nodes. Each node stores a value and a pointer to the next node. Unlike an array, there is no instant “jump to index 50”; you follow links one by one.</p><p>Think of a treasure hunt where every note tells you where the next note is. If you tear up the current note before copying its next location, you lose the rest of the hunt. That is exactly why pointer-update order matters.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Manipulate links without losing nodes. Draw old and new arrows before assigning pointers.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Reverse A→B→C. Save B before changing A.next. Make A→None. Move to B, save C, make B→A. Move to C, make C→B. The crucial habit is SAVE NEXT before overwriting the arrow.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Copy all values into an array, solve there, then rebuild. It often uses unnecessary O(n) space and ignores the pointer skill the interviewer wants.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Preserve the next pointer before rewiring the current link. Use dummy nodes to remove head special cases. Use fast/slow pointers when relative position matters.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Most list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Linked Lists to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 584}, "p6-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Reverse or reorder nodes</li><li>Delete/insert without shifting an array</li><li>Cycle or midpoint detection</li><li>Merge sorted linked streams</li><li>Need O(1) operations plus recency ordering such as LRU cache</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Anything with “list” in English — it may mean an array/list container</li><li>Need random index access — linked lists are poor at it</li><li>Need sorted searching — binary search is not efficient on a singly linked list</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Contiguous array/string range → Sliding Window</li><li>Repeated best among many streams → Heap may be the main pattern even if nodes are linked</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Most list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Preserve the next pointer before rewiring the current link. Use dummy nodes to remove head special cases. Use fast/slow pointers when relative position matters.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Manipulate links without losing nodes. Draw old and new arrows before assigning pointers.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find a target quickly in a sorted linked list” is not a binary-search win because finding the middle is not O(1). Sequential traversal remains O(n).</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Reverse or reorder nodes, Delete/insert without shifting an array?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “At every step, the processed portion is correctly linked, and you still hold a valid pointer to every unprocessed node you will need later.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 598}, "p6-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Copy all values into an array, solve there, then rebuild. It often uses unnecessary O(n) space and ignores the pointer skill the interviewer wants.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Preserve the next pointer before rewiring the current link. Use dummy nodes to remove head special cases. Use fast/slow pointers when relative position matters.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>At every step, the processed portion is correctly linked, and you still hold a valid pointer to every unprocessed node you will need later.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">prev = None\ncur = head\nwhile cur:\n    nxt = cur.next      # save the future\n    cur.next = prev     # reverse one arrow\n    prev = cur\n    cur = nxt\nreturn prev</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Most list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty head</li><li>one node</li><li>two nodes</li><li>cycle</li><li>delete head/tail</li><li>even vs odd length</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Overwrite cur.next before saving it</li><li>Forget dummy node and create many head special cases</li><li>Fast pointer jumps without checking fast and fast.next</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 510}, "p6-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Reverse list</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Merge two sorted lists with dummy head</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Remove nth from end using fixed fast/slow gap</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Reorder = split + reverse + merge</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Copy random pointer = mapping/interweaving</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>LRU = hash map + doubly linked list</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Singly or doubly linked?</li><li>Can head be null?</li><li>May I allocate new nodes?</li><li>Must I preserve original nodes and O(1) extra space?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Overwrite cur.next before saving it</li><li>Forget dummy node and create many head special cases</li><li>Fast pointer jumps without checking fast and fast.next</li><li>Return old head after reversal</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Manipulate links without losing nodes. Draw old and new arrows before assigning pointers.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Copy all values into an array, solve there, then rebuild. It often uses unnecessary O(n) space and ignores the pointer skill the interviewer wants. The useful idea is: Preserve the next pointer before rewiring the current link. Use dummy nodes to remove head special cases. Use fast/slow pointers when relative position matters.</p></section>", "wordCount": 537}, "p6-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Two Pointers</b></td><td>fast/slow is a pointer technique but node links make mutation/order the main challenge</td></tr><tr><td><b>Heap</b></td><td>merge k sorted lists chooses best head among k streams</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hash Map</b></td><td>random-pointer copies/LRU use maps to jump to nodes quickly</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Find a target quickly in a sorted linked list” is not a binary-search win because finding the middle is not O(1). Sequential traversal remains O(n).</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Reverse list</li><li>Merge two sorted lists with dummy head</li><li>Remove nth from end using fixed fast/slow gap</li><li>Reorder = split + reverse + merge</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Reverse or reorder nodes</li><li>Delete/insert without shifting an array</li><li>Cycle or midpoint detection</li><li>Merge sorted linked streams</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Anything with “list” in English — it may mean an array/list container</li><li>Need random index access — linked lists are poor at it</li><li>Need sorted searching — binary search is not efficient on a singly linked list</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find a target quickly in a sorted linked list” is not a binary-search win because finding the middle is not O(1). Sequential traversal remains O(n).</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Reverse a singly linked chain in place.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Determine whether a linked list contains a cycle.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find a value in a sorted array by halving the search space.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Build an LRU cache with O(1) get/put.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 520}, "p6-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard linked-list problems are often compositions of simple moves: find midpoint, reverse half, merge; or isolate groups, reverse each group, reconnect boundaries. Break the problem into arrow transformations.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Dummy nodes simplify merges/deletions</li><li>Fast/slow gap solves kth-from-end in one pass</li><li>Cycle entry can be found after Floyd collision with a reset</li><li>LRU combines two structures because one structure cannot provide both O(1) lookup and O(1) recency updates</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Draw arrows. Seriously—linked lists punish “I can visualize it in my head.”</li><li>Name temporary pointer nxt every time you rewire</li><li>For deletion/merge, ask whether a dummy head eliminates a special case</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>At every step, the processed portion is correctly linked, and you still hold a valid pointer to every unprocessed node you will need later.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Most list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 506}, "p6-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Linked Lists\n\nCORE → Manipulate links without losing nodes. Draw old and new arrows before assigning pointers.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Reverse or reorder nodes\n- Delete/insert without shifting an array\n- Cycle or midpoint detection\n\nINVARIANT\nAt every step, the processed portion is correctly linked, and you still hold a valid pointer to every unprocessed node you will need later.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nMost list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nLINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Reverse a singly linked chain in place.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Determine whether a linked list contains a cycle.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find a value in a sorted array by halving the search space.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Build an LRU cache with O(1) get/put.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Most list passes are O(n) because every node is visited once. Reversal uses O(1) extra space iteratively. Recursive reversal uses O(n) call-stack space. Heap-based merge-k-lists is O(N log k).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">prev = None\ncur = head\nwhile cur:\n    nxt = cur.next      # save the future\n    cur.next = prev     # reverse one arrow\n    prev = cur\n    cur = nxt\nreturn prev</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Linked Lists come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">before: A → B → C → None\n        prev  cur\n\nsave nxt=B\nrewire A → None\nmove: prev=A, cur=B</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FAST/SLOW\nslow: 1 step\nfast: 2 steps\n\ncycle? relative gap eventually wraps to 0 → collision\nno cycle? fast reaches None</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> LINKED LIST = protect the future pointer before changing the present arrow.</p></section>", "wordCount": 521}, "p7-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Return maximum depth of a binary tree.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find kth smallest in a BST.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A tree is a hierarchy. A node can have children, which can have their own children. Recursion is natural because “solve this tree” often means “solve the left smaller tree, solve the right smaller tree, combine.”</p><p>Think of folders inside folders. To count every file, you can enter one folder deeply before returning (DFS), or inspect all folders at the current depth before the next depth (BFS).</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Define what one recursive call means. Choose DFS when child results combine naturally; choose BFS when level/distance order matters.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Maximum depth: define dfs(node) = depth of subtree rooted at node. Empty node returns 0. A real node returns 1 + max(depth(left), depth(right)). The code is tiny because the contract is clear.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Repeatedly recompute subtree height/size for every node, or write ad-hoc nested logic per level. This can turn an O(n) traversal into O(n²).</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>If every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Tree Traversal & Recursion to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 585}, "p7-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Hierarchy/parent-child structure</li><li>Answer depends on subtree information</li><li>Need preorder/inorder/postorder behavior</li><li>“level”, “minimum number of edges”, or right-side view → BFS</li><li>Grid/graph can sometimes be treated as a traversal problem too</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any nested data</li><li>Need ordering of values rather than structural traversal</li><li>Recursion appears in a possible solution but is not the core pattern</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>BST ordering can enable specialized bounds/search rather than generic traversal</li><li>Weighted shortest path needs Dijkstra, not plain BFS</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: If every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Define what one recursive call means. Choose DFS when child results combine naturally; choose BFS when level/distance order matters.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find the kth smallest value in a BST” is a tree problem, but the BST inorder ordering is the key extra structure; generic DFS alone misses the sharper pattern.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Hierarchy/parent-child structure, Answer depends on subtree information?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “A recursive call must have a precise contract: given this node, it returns/updates exactly one defined meaning, and all child calls obey the same contract.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 594}, "p7-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Repeatedly recompute subtree height/size for every node, or write ad-hoc nested logic per level. This can turn an O(n) traversal into O(n²).</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>A recursive call must have a precise contract: given this node, it returns/updates exactly one defined meaning, and all child calls obey the same contract.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def dfs(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return BASE\n    left = dfs(node.left)\n    right = dfs(node.right)\n    return combine(node, left, right)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>If every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty tree</li><li>single node</li><li>fully skewed</li><li>duplicate values</li><li>very deep tree</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>No clear base case</li><li>Confuse node count vs edge count</li><li>Use global state without resetting</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Repeatedly recompute subtree height/size for every node, or write ad-hoc nested logic per level. This can turn an O(n) traversal into O(n²). The useful idea is: Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p></section>", "wordCount": 555}, "p7-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Maximum depth: return height</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Diameter: child heights + global/local path</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Same Tree: compare paired nodes</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Level order: queue by levels</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Right Side View: take last/first visible node per level</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Binary tree or arbitrary children?</li><li>Can node values repeat?</li><li>Need node count, edge count, path, or value?</li><li>Is recursion depth safe for constraints?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>No clear base case</li><li>Confuse node count vs edge count</li><li>Use global state without resetting</li><li>Recompute subtree results repeatedly</li><li>Choose BFS when DFS return values would be simpler</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Define what one recursive call means. Choose DFS when child results combine naturally; choose BFS when level/distance order matters.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Repeatedly recompute subtree height/size for every node, or write ad-hoc nested logic per level. This can turn an O(n) traversal into O(n²). The useful idea is: Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p></section>", "wordCount": 537}, "p7-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>BST logic</b></td><td>ordering property changes what branches are possible</td></tr><tr><td><b>Graph traversal</b></td><td>must manage arbitrary cycles/visited state</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tree DP</b></td><td>returned child values combine into optimization/global answers</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Find the kth smallest value in a BST” is a tree problem, but the BST inorder ordering is the key extra structure; generic DFS alone misses the sharper pattern.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Maximum depth: return height</li><li>Diameter: child heights + global/local path</li><li>Same Tree: compare paired nodes</li><li>Level order: queue by levels</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Hierarchy/parent-child structure</li><li>Answer depends on subtree information</li><li>Need preorder/inorder/postorder behavior</li><li>“level”, “minimum number of edges”, or right-side view → BFS</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any nested data</li><li>Need ordering of values rather than structural traversal</li><li>Recursion appears in a possible solution but is not the core pattern</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Find the kth smallest value in a BST” is a tree problem, but the BST inorder ordering is the key extra structure; generic DFS alone misses the sharper pattern.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Return maximum depth of a binary tree.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return values level by level.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find kth smallest in a BST.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Check whether two trees have identical structure and values.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Repeatedly recompute subtree height/size for every node, or write ad-hoc nested logic per level. This can turn an O(n) traversal into O(n²). The useful idea is: Let each node be handled once. Ask children for exactly the piece of information the parent needs, then combine those returned values.</p></section>", "wordCount": 556}, "p7-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard tree questions often require two meanings: what this node returns upward and what global answer can pass through this node. Separate those two ideas explicitly.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Postorder is natural when parent needs child results first</li><li>Preorder is natural when parent state must be passed down</li><li>BFS naturally finds shortest unweighted tree depth</li><li>Many “hard” tree problems are just a carefully defined return contract plus a global answer</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Before coding write: “dfs(node) returns ____.”</li><li>Draw the base case value and check combine logic against a leaf</li><li>If the problem says “per level,” consider BFS immediately</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>A recursive call must have a precise contract: given this node, it returns/updates exactly one defined meaning, and all child calls obey the same contract.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>If every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 513}, "p7-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Tree Traversal & Recursion\n\nCORE → Define what one recursive call means. Choose DFS when child results combine naturally; choose BFS when level/distance order matters.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Hierarchy/parent-child structure\n- Answer depends on subtree information\n- Need preorder/inorder/postorder behavior\n\nINVARIANT\nA recursive call must have a precise contract: given this node, it returns/updates exactly one defined meaning, and all child calls obey the same contract.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nIf every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nTREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Return maximum depth of a binary tree.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return values level by level.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find kth smallest in a BST.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Check whether two trees have identical structure and values.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: If every node is visited once and each visit does O(1) work, time is O(n). Recursive DFS space is O(h) where h is tree height: O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed. BFS queue can hold O(width) nodes.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def dfs(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return BASE\n    left = dfs(node.left)\n    right = dfs(node.right)\n    return combine(node, left, right)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Tree Traversal & Recursion come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        A\n      /   \\\n     B     C\n    / \\     \\\n   D   E     F\n\nDFS: A B D E C F\nBFS: A | B C | D E F</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PARENT asks children:\n        “what is your answer?”\n        /              \\\n   left answer      right answer\n        \\              /\n          COMBINE HERE</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> TREE RECURSION = define one node’s contract, trust children, combine once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 525}, "p8-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Validate whether a binary tree obeys global BST rules.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return tree values level by level.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A Binary Search Tree adds an ordering rule: everything in the left subtree is smaller (under the usual definition) and everything in the right is larger. Tree construction and tree DP add state that must flow through recursion.</p><p>A BST is like a phonebook decision tree: smaller names go left, larger names go right. Tree DP is like each department reporting a summary upward so headquarters can compute a larger result.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Exploit ordering/bounds, reconstruct structure from traversal boundaries, and distinguish “value returned to parent” from “best answer anywhere below.”</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Validate BST: checking only node.left < node < node.right is insufficient. A deep right-descendant of the root’s left child must still be < root. Carry bounds: left subtree gets (low, root), right gets (root, high).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Search both subtrees even when BST bounds prove one side impossible; rebuild arrays repeatedly during construction; recompute subtree path values many times.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Carry legal value bounds down the tree. Use traversal indices/maps instead of slicing arrays. For path DP, have each child return one extendable contribution and update a separate global best.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Typical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain BST, Tree Construction & Tree DP to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section>", "wordCount": 576}, "p8-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>BST ordering or validation</li><li>kth smallest/largest in BST</li><li>Construct tree from preorder/inorder</li><li>Serialize/deserialize structure</li><li>Path answer combines child contributions</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any binary tree</li><li>Values happen to look sorted in one example</li><li>Need level-order output — generic BFS is enough</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>No BST ordering → do not prune branches based on value</li><li>Graph with cycles → tree assumptions fail</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Typical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Carry legal value bounds down the tree. Use traversal indices/maps instead of slicing arrays. For path DP, have each child return one extendable contribution and update a separate global best.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Exploit ordering/bounds, reconstruct structure from traversal boundaries, and distinguish “value returned to parent” from “best answer anywhere below.”</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Level-order traversal of an arbitrary binary tree needs no BST ordering or tree DP. Use a plain BFS queue.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: BST ordering or validation, kth smallest/largest in BST?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “Every recursive call carries valid state for that subtree: allowed value range, traversal boundaries, or a precisely defined contribution that can extend to the parent.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section>", "wordCount": 587}, "p8-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Search both subtrees even when BST bounds prove one side impossible; rebuild arrays repeatedly during construction; recompute subtree path values many times.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Carry legal value bounds down the tree. Use traversal indices/maps instead of slicing arrays. For path DP, have each child return one extendable contribution and update a separate global best.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>Every recursive call carries valid state for that subtree: allowed value range, traversal boundaries, or a precisely defined contribution that can extend to the parent.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def valid(node, low, high):\n    if not node: return True\n    if not (low < node.val < high): return False\n    return (valid(node.left, low, node.val) and\n            valid(node.right, node.val, high))</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Typical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>skewed BST</li><li>duplicate values</li><li>negative path values</li><li>empty serialization</li><li>single node</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Validate only parent-child relationship</li><li>Use array slicing in construction and accidentally create O(n²)</li><li>Return a two-branch path upward when a parent can only extend one branch</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section>", "wordCount": 509}, "p8-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>BST validation with bounds</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Kth smallest via inorder rank</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Construct from preorder + inorder index boundaries</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Serialize using explicit null markers</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Maximum path sum with local return + global candidate</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Strict BST or duplicates allowed on a chosen side?</li><li>Are node values unique for reconstruction?</li><li>What traversal orders are provided?</li><li>Does a path have to start/end at root or can it be anywhere?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Validate only parent-child relationship</li><li>Use array slicing in construction and accidentally create O(n²)</li><li>Return a two-branch path upward when a parent can only extend one branch</li><li>Omit null markers when serializing shape</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Exploit ordering/bounds, reconstruct structure from traversal boundaries, and distinguish “value returned to parent” from “best answer anywhere below.”</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Search both subtrees even when BST bounds prove one side impossible; rebuild arrays repeatedly during construction; recompute subtree path values many times. The useful idea is: Carry legal value bounds down the tree. Use traversal indices/maps instead of slicing arrays. For path DP, have each child return one extendable contribution and update a separate global best.</p></section>", "wordCount": 565}, "p8-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Generic Tree DFS</b></td><td>no ordering/state beyond children is needed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Binary Search</b></td><td>search space is an index/answer range rather than a pointer-based tree</td></tr><tr><td><b>1-D/2-D DP</b></td><td>tree DP states are attached to nodes/edges instead of array indices</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>Level-order traversal of an arbitrary binary tree needs no BST ordering or tree DP. Use a plain BFS queue.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>BST validation with bounds</li><li>Kth smallest via inorder rank</li><li>Construct from preorder + inorder index boundaries</li><li>Serialize using explicit null markers</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>BST ordering or validation</li><li>kth smallest/largest in BST</li><li>Construct tree from preorder/inorder</li><li>Serialize/deserialize structure</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any binary tree</li><li>Values happen to look sorted in one example</li><li>Need level-order output — generic BFS is enough</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Level-order traversal of an arbitrary binary tree needs no BST ordering or tree DP. Use a plain BFS queue.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Validate whether a binary tree obeys global BST rules.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return kth smallest BST value.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Return tree values level by level.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find maximum path sum that may pass through any node.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Search both subtrees even when BST bounds prove one side impossible; rebuild arrays repeatedly during construction; recompute subtree path values many times. The useful idea is: Carry legal value bounds down the tree. Use traversal indices/maps instead of slicing arrays. For path DP, have each child return one extendable contribution and update a separate global best.</p></section>", "wordCount": 552}, "p8-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>The hard part is state semantics. If you can say exactly what low/high, preorder index, or returned path value means, the recursion becomes mechanical.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Bounds are safer than local neighbor checks</li><li>Inorder traversal of BST is sorted</li><li>Preorder identifies root first; inorder splits left/right</li><li>Tree DP often has “return one branch, evaluate two branches” structure</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write the recursive state tuple beside the function parameters</li><li>For tree DP, create two labels: RETURN UP and UPDATE GLOBAL</li><li>When reconstructing, avoid copying arrays; use index ranges</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>Every recursive call carries valid state for that subtree: allowed value range, traversal boundaries, or a precisely defined contribution that can extend to the parent.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Typical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section>", "wordCount": 503}, "p8-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: BST, Tree Construction & Tree DP\n\nCORE → Exploit ordering/bounds, reconstruct structure from traversal boundaries, and distinguish “value returned to parent” from “best answer anywhere below.”\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- BST ordering or validation\n- kth smallest/largest in BST\n- Construct tree from preorder/inorder\n\nINVARIANT\nEvery recursive call carries valid state for that subtree: allowed value range, traversal boundaries, or a precisely defined contribution that can extend to the parent.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nTypical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nBST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Validate whether a binary tree obeys global BST rules.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return kth smallest BST value.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Return tree values level by level.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find maximum path sum that may pass through any node.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Typical correct traversals are O(n). Validation uses O(h) stack. Construction can be O(n) with an inorder index map, but O(n²) if you repeatedly search/slice. BST search is O(h): O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def valid(node, low, high):\n    if not node: return True\n    if not (low < node.val < high): return False\n    return (valid(node.left, low, node.val) and\n            valid(node.right, node.val, high))</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should BST, Tree Construction & Tree DP come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">          10   allowed (-∞,+∞)\n        /    \\\n       5      15\n   (-∞,10)   (10,+∞)\n        \\\n         12  ← INVALID: 12 is inside left subtree of 10</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">TREE DP\nchild returns: “best path I can EXTEND upward”\nnode computes: left + node + right  ← candidate GLOBAL best\nnode returns: node + max(left,right)</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BST/TREE DP = carry legal state down; return one well-defined summary up.</p></section>", "wordCount": 526}, "p9-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Continuously report kth largest after every insertion.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Minimum number of rooms for overlapping meetings.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A heap is a structure that quickly gives you the smallest or largest current candidate. Interval problems often become easy after sorting by start/end time, then using a heap to track active work.</p><p>Imagine an emergency room. Patients arrive over time, but you repeatedly need the most urgent active patient. A normal list would search everyone each time; a priority queue keeps the best next choice ready.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> When the set of candidates changes and you repeatedly need the best one, maintain that frontier with a heap. For intervals, sort events so time order is explicit.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Meeting Rooms II: sort meetings by start time. Keep a min-heap of end times for active rooms. Before a meeting starts, pop rooms whose end <= new start. Push new end. Maximum heap size is rooms needed.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Repeatedly scan all active candidates to find minimum/maximum. If you do that n times over n candidates, O(n²) appears.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> A heap spends O(log k) to insert/remove but keeps the best candidate at the top in O(1). Sorting intervals once can transform a story into a chronological sweep.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Heap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Heaps, Intervals & Scheduling to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section>", "wordCount": 585}, "p9-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Top K / kth largest/smallest</li><li>Repeatedly choose current min/max</li><li>Merge k sorted streams</li><li>Streaming median</li><li>Meetings/tasks overlap and active items expire</li><li>Need best eligible candidate as time advances</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Need the single global max once — one linear scan is simpler</li><li>Input is merely “sorted”</li><li>Any scheduling story; some are pure greedy without a heap</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Most recently unresolved → Stack</li><li>Membership lookup → Hash Map</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Heap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>A heap spends O(log k) to insert/remove but keeps the best candidate at the top in O(1). Sorting intervals once can transform a story into a chronological sweep.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>When the set of candidates changes and you repeatedly need the best one, maintain that frontier with a heap. For intervals, sort events so time order is explicit.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Maximum subarray sum” asks for a best value but does not require repeatedly selecting among changing candidates. Kadane/greedy-DP is the real pattern.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Top K / kth largest/smallest, Repeatedly choose current min/max?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The heap contains exactly the active/relevant candidates, ordered by the key needed for the next decision; stale/finished items are removed when they can no longer matter.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section>", "wordCount": 596}, "p9-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Repeatedly scan all active candidates to find minimum/maximum. If you do that n times over n candidates, O(n²) appears.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. A heap spends O(log k) to insert/remove but keeps the best candidate at the top in O(1). Sorting intervals once can transform a story into a chronological sweep.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The heap contains exactly the active/relevant candidates, ordered by the key needed for the next decision; stale/finished items are removed when they can no longer matter.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">heap = []\nfor item in ordered_items:\n    while heap and is_stale(heap[0], item):\n        heappop(heap)\n    heappush(heap, key(item))\n    answer = update(answer, heap)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Heap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty heap</li><li>ties</li><li>stale entries</li><li>k > n</li><li>touching intervals count as overlap or not</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Use max-heap when min-heap of top-k is simpler</li><li>Forget to remove stale intervals</li><li>Store full objects when only a key+id is needed</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section>", "wordCount": 509}, "p9-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Kth largest stream: keep min-heap of size k</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>K closest points: heap/selection</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Merge k lists: heap of current heads</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Median stream: two heaps</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Meeting Rooms II: min-heap of ending times</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Minimum Interval Query: sort queries + heap active intervals</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Do I need min or max?</li><li>Is K small relative to n?</li><li>Can items become stale as time advances?</li><li>Does the answer need current best repeatedly or only once?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Use max-heap when min-heap of top-k is simpler</li><li>Forget to remove stale intervals</li><li>Store full objects when only a key+id is needed</li><li>Re-sort inside the loop</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>When the set of candidates changes and you repeatedly need the best one, maintain that frontier with a heap. For intervals, sort events so time order is explicit.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Repeatedly scan all active candidates to find minimum/maximum. If you do that n times over n candidates, O(n²) appears. The useful idea is: A heap spends O(log k) to insert/remove but keeps the best candidate at the top in O(1). Sorting intervals once can transform a story into a chronological sweep.</p></section>", "wordCount": 559}, "p9-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Greedy</b></td><td>if one sorted local choice is enough and no dynamic candidate set is needed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Stack</b></td><td>last unresolved candidate matters, not global best</td></tr><tr><td><b>Binary Search</b></td><td>search a monotonic answer rather than maintain active best items</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Maximum subarray sum” asks for a best value but does not require repeatedly selecting among changing candidates. Kadane/greedy-DP is the real pattern.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Kth largest stream: keep min-heap of size k</li><li>K closest points: heap/selection</li><li>Merge k lists: heap of current heads</li><li>Median stream: two heaps</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Top K / kth largest/smallest</li><li>Repeatedly choose current min/max</li><li>Merge k sorted streams</li><li>Streaming median</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Need the single global max once — one linear scan is simpler</li><li>Input is merely “sorted”</li><li>Any scheduling story; some are pure greedy without a heap</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Maximum subarray sum” asks for a best value but does not require repeatedly selecting among changing candidates. Kadane/greedy-DP is the real pattern.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Continuously report kth largest after every insertion.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return whether any duplicate exists.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Minimum number of rooms for overlapping meetings.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Return median after each new number arrives.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Repeatedly scan all active candidates to find minimum/maximum. If you do that n times over n candidates, O(n²) appears. The useful idea is: A heap spends O(log k) to insert/remove but keeps the best candidate at the top in O(1). Sorting intervals once can transform a story into a chronological sweep.</p></section>", "wordCount": 553}, "p9-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard heap questions hide candidate eligibility. Sort one dimension, then let the heap handle the other dimension—often size, end time, distance, or cost.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Two heaps balance lower/upper halves for median</li><li>Offline query sorting lets you add candidates once as queries increase</li><li>Lazy deletion is useful when arbitrary heap removal is expensive</li><li>Quickselect may beat a heap for one static kth-element query</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Say what heap[0] means in English</li><li>Write “push when ___; pop when ___”</li><li>If you only need one maximum once, do not reach for a heap</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The heap contains exactly the active/relevant candidates, ordered by the key needed for the next decision; stale/finished items are removed when they can no longer matter.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Heap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section>", "wordCount": 505}, "p9-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Heaps, Intervals & Scheduling\n\nCORE → When the set of candidates changes and you repeatedly need the best one, maintain that frontier with a heap. For intervals, sort events so time order is explicit.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Top K / kth largest/smallest\n- Repeatedly choose current min/max\n- Merge k sorted streams\n\nINVARIANT\nThe heap contains exactly the active/relevant candidates, ordered by the key needed for the next decision; stale/finished items are removed when they can no longer matter.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nHeap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nHEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Continuously report kth largest after every insertion.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Return whether any duplicate exists.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Minimum number of rooms for overlapping meetings.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Return median after each new number arrives.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Heap push/pop is O(log k) for heap size k. Processing n items is commonly O(n log k), or O(n log n) when k can be n. Sorting intervals costs O(n log n). Heap space is O(k).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">heap = []\nfor item in ordered_items:\n    while heap and is_stale(heap[0], item):\n        heappop(heap)\n    heappush(heap, key(item))\n    answer = update(answer, heap)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Heaps, Intervals & Scheduling come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">time →\nA: [------]\nB:    [----]\nC:          [---]\n\nactive end-times heap at B start: [A_end]\nB overlaps → push B_end → 2 rooms</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CANDIDATES change over time\n        ↓ push/pop O(log k)\n   [ PRIORITY HEAP ]\n        ↓ top O(1)\n      BEST NEXT</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> HEAP = dynamic candidate set + repeatedly need the best next item.</p></section>", "wordCount": 537}, "p10-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Generate all subsets.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find shortest path in an unweighted graph.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>Backtracking explores a decision tree: choose something, move forward, and if that path cannot or should not continue, undo the choice and try another branch. A trie is a prefix tree that makes shared string prefixes cheap to test.</p><p>Imagine trying keys on a lock. Pick a key choice, continue; if it leads to a dead end, put everything back exactly as it was and try the next choice. A trie is like a dictionary organized by letter-by-letter hallways.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Define state, choices, constraints, base case, and undo. Prune as early as possible when a partial choice can never lead to a valid answer.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Subsets of [1,2]: each element has two choices—take or skip. The decision tree has 2² leaves: [], [1], [2], [1,2]. For permutations, available choices shrink after each level.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Complexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Backtracking & Trie Search to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section>", "wordCount": 560}, "p10-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Generate all subsets/permutations/combinations</li><li>“all possible” arrangements under constraints</li><li>Path/word search with choose/undo</li><li>N-Queens / Sudoku-like placement</li><li>Many words share prefixes and grid search should stop when prefix impossible</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any recursion</li><li>Need one optimal numeric value — DP may avoid enumerating outputs</li><li>Graph DFS without undoable choices</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Repeated overlapping subproblems with same state → DP/memoization</li><li>Need simple connectivity → Graph DFS/BFS</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Complexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Define state, choices, constraints, base case, and undo. Prune as early as possible when a partial choice can never lead to a valid answer.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Can I reach every node in this graph?” uses DFS recursion, but there is no choice/undo tree. It is graph traversal, not backtracking.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Generate all subsets/permutations/combinations, “all possible” arrangements under constraints?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The current path/state represents exactly the choices made along this recursion branch; before returning to the caller, every temporary choice is undone.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section>", "wordCount": 564}, "p10-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The current path/state represents exactly the choices made along this recursion branch; before returning to the caller, every temporary choice is undone.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def backtrack(start, path):\n    if complete(path):\n        answer.append(path.copy())\n        return\n    for choice in choices(start):\n        if not allowed(choice, path):\n            continue\n        path.append(choice)\n        backtrack(next_state, path)\n        path.pop()   # undo</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Complexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty choice set</li><li>duplicate candidates</li><li>target becomes negative</li><li>visited cell restore</li><li>deep recursion</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Forget path.copy() and all answers point to same list</li><li>Forget undo step</li><li>Handle duplicates at wrong recursion depth</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes. The useful idea is: Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section>", "wordCount": 529}, "p10-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Subsets: choose/skip</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Permutations: choose any unused item</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Combination Sum: choices with target remaining</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Palindrome Partitioning: split only on valid palindrome</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Word Search: grid path + visited undo</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Word Search II: trie prunes prefixes</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Need all solutions, count, or just existence?</li><li>Can values repeat?</li><li>Can the same input item be reused?</li><li>What partial condition lets us prune?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Forget path.copy() and all answers point to same list</li><li>Forget undo step</li><li>Handle duplicates at wrong recursion depth</li><li>Explore a branch after it is already impossible</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Define state, choices, constraints, base case, and undo. Prune as early as possible when a partial choice can never lead to a valid answer.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes. The useful idea is: Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section>", "wordCount": 515}, "p10-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>DP</b></td><td>same state repeats and you need count/best, not enumerate every path</td></tr><tr><td><b>Graph DFS</b></td><td>visit each node/component; no choice tree/undo semantics</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greedy</b></td><td>one provably safe local choice avoids exploring alternatives</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Can I reach every node in this graph?” uses DFS recursion, but there is no choice/undo tree. It is graph traversal, not backtracking.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Subsets: choose/skip</li><li>Permutations: choose any unused item</li><li>Combination Sum: choices with target remaining</li><li>Palindrome Partitioning: split only on valid palindrome</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Generate all subsets/permutations/combinations</li><li>“all possible” arrangements under constraints</li><li>Path/word search with choose/undo</li><li>N-Queens / Sudoku-like placement</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any recursion</li><li>Need one optimal numeric value — DP may avoid enumerating outputs</li><li>Graph DFS without undoable choices</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Can I reach every node in this graph?” uses DFS recursion, but there is no choice/undo tree. It is graph traversal, not backtracking.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Generate all subsets.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find a word in a board without reusing a cell.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find shortest path in an unweighted graph.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Return all unique combinations summing to target.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes. The useful idea is: Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section>", "wordCount": 519}, "p10-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard backtracking is mostly about pruning and state representation. A powerful prefix test, remaining-capacity bound, or duplicate rule can remove most branches before they grow.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Sort + skip duplicates at the same tree depth</li><li>Trie pruning turns “try every word” into prefix-aware search</li><li>Bitmasks can encode used choices compactly</li><li>Memoization can hybridize backtracking when states repeat</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write five words before code: STATE, CHOICES, VALID?, DONE?, UNDO</li><li>Draw 2 levels of the decision tree</li><li>If output count itself is exponential, exponential runtime may be unavoidable</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The current path/state represents exactly the choices made along this recursion branch; before returning to the caller, every temporary choice is undone.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Complexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Generate every possible sequence blindly, even branches that already violate constraints. The search tree explodes. The useful idea is: Backtracking is controlled brute force: stop invalid branches early, avoid duplicate choices, and mutate/undo state instead of copying huge structures when safe.</p></section>", "wordCount": 535}, "p10-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Backtracking & Trie Search\n\nCORE → Define state, choices, constraints, base case, and undo. Prune as early as possible when a partial choice can never lead to a valid answer.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Generate all subsets/permutations/combinations\n- “all possible” arrangements under constraints\n- Path/word search with choose/undo\n\nINVARIANT\nThe current path/state represents exactly the choices made along this recursion branch; before returning to the caller, every temporary choice is undone.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nComplexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nBACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Generate all subsets.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find a word in a board without reusing a cell.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find shortest path in an unweighted graph.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Return all unique combinations summing to target.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Complexity is often exponential because the number of possible outputs is exponential. Think branching factor^depth, then multiply by cost to copy/build each answer. Pruning improves practical work but often not worst-case class.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">def backtrack(start, path):\n    if complete(path):\n        answer.append(path.copy())\n        return\n    for choice in choices(start):\n        if not allowed(choice, path):\n            continue\n        path.append(choice)\n        backtrack(next_state, path)\n        path.pop()   # undo</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Backtracking & Trie Search come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">              []\n          / take1  \\ skip1\n        [1]          []\n      /   \\        /  \\\n   [1,2] [1]      [2]  []</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">CHOOSE → EXPLORE → UNDO\n   ↑                  │\n   └──── try next ────┘\n\nPRUNE whenever partial state can no longer succeed</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> BACKTRACKING = choose, explore, undo; prune dead branches early.</p></section>", "wordCount": 509}, "p11-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count islands in a grid.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find cheapest weighted route.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A graph is just things (nodes) connected to other things (edges). A grid can also be a graph: each cell is a node and neighboring cells are edges. DFS explores deeply; BFS explores in layers.</p><p>Think of cities and roads. “Can I reach city B?” is connectivity. “How many separate island groups?” is components. “Fewest roads in an unweighted map?” is BFS layers.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Translate the story into nodes, edges, and visited state. Choose DFS for reachability/components and BFS when shortest unweighted distance/layers matter.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Number of Islands: every land cell is a node; edges connect up/down/left/right land. Scan grid. When you find unvisited land, increment island count and DFS/BFS to mark the whole component.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Start a fresh search from every node without visited memory, repeatedly exploring the same region, or recursively wander cycles forever.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Graph Traversal to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 573}, "p11-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Connectivity, components, islands</li><li>Grid cells with neighbor movement</li><li>Shortest number of steps in unweighted graph</li><li>Clone network</li><li>Dependency cycle/topological prerequisites</li><li>Flood fill / spread over time</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any matrix</li><li>Any tree (trees are graphs but simpler because no cycles)</li><li>Weighted shortest path — plain BFS is wrong</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Weighted positive edges → Dijkstra</li><li>Enumerate decision combinations → Backtracking</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Translate the story into nodes, edges, and visited state. Choose DFS for reachability/components and BFS when shortest unweighted distance/layers matter.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Cheapest flight with ticket prices” is a graph, but edges are weighted. Plain BFS layers do not guarantee cheapest cost; advanced shortest-path reasoning is needed.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Connectivity, components, islands, Grid cells with neighbor movement?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “Visited means the node has already been discovered under the search semantics and should not be re-added/reprocessed unnecessarily.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 571}, "p11-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Start a fresh search from every node without visited memory, repeatedly exploring the same region, or recursively wander cycles forever.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>Visited means the node has already been discovered under the search semantics and should not be re-added/reprocessed unnecessarily.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">from collections import deque\nq = deque([start])\nseen = {start}\nwhile q:\n    node = q.popleft()\n    for nei in graph[node]:\n        if nei not in seen:\n            seen.add(nei)\n            q.append(nei)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>disconnected graph</li><li>self-loop</li><li>duplicate edges</li><li>empty graph</li><li>isolated node</li><li>large recursion depth</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Mark visited too late and enqueue duplicates</li><li>Use BFS for weighted shortest path</li><li>Forget disconnected components require multiple starts</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Start a fresh search from every node without visited memory, repeatedly exploring the same region, or recursively wander cycles forever. The useful idea is: Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p></section>", "wordCount": 557}, "p11-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Islands/components</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Clone Graph with old→new mapping</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Rotting Oranges multi-source BFS</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Pacific Atlantic reverse reachability</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Course Schedule cycle/topological ordering</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Word Ladder BFS over transformations</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Directed or undirected?</li><li>Weighted or unweighted?</li><li>What counts as a neighbor?</li><li>Can a node be revisited under a different state?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Mark visited too late and enqueue duplicates</li><li>Use BFS for weighted shortest path</li><li>Forget disconnected components require multiple starts</li><li>Mutate grid without knowing whether allowed</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Translate the story into nodes, edges, and visited state. Choose DFS for reachability/components and BFS when shortest unweighted distance/layers matter.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Start a fresh search from every node without visited memory, repeatedly exploring the same region, or recursively wander cycles forever. The useful idea is: Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p></section>", "wordCount": 532}, "p11-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Tree traversal</b></td><td>no arbitrary cycles; parent relationship simplifies visited logic</td></tr><tr><td><b>Advanced Graph/Dijkstra</b></td><td>edge weights matter</td></tr><tr><td><b>Backtracking</b></td><td>paths are choices to enumerate, not just nodes to visit once</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>“Cheapest flight with ticket prices” is a graph, but edges are weighted. Plain BFS layers do not guarantee cheapest cost; advanced shortest-path reasoning is needed.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Islands/components</li><li>Clone Graph with old→new mapping</li><li>Rotting Oranges multi-source BFS</li><li>Pacific Atlantic reverse reachability</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Connectivity, components, islands</li><li>Grid cells with neighbor movement</li><li>Shortest number of steps in unweighted graph</li><li>Clone network</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any matrix</li><li>Any tree (trees are graphs but simpler because no cycles)</li><li>Weighted shortest path — plain BFS is wrong</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>“Cheapest flight with ticket prices” is a graph, but edges are weighted. Plain BFS layers do not guarantee cheapest cost; advanced shortest-path reasoning is needed.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count islands in a grid.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find shortest number of moves in an unweighted maze.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find cheapest weighted route.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Detect whether course prerequisites contain a cycle.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Start a fresh search from every node without visited memory, repeatedly exploring the same region, or recursively wander cycles forever. The useful idea is: Mark nodes when discovered/visited so each node and edge is processed a small number of times. BFS queue preserves distance layers automatically.</p></section>", "wordCount": 539}, "p11-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard graph problems disguise the graph. Words can be nodes, states can be nodes, and legal transformations can be edges. The breakthrough is often defining the graph correctly before choosing BFS/DFS.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Multi-source BFS starts all sources at distance 0</li><li>Reverse edges/reachability can make “can reach X” easier</li><li>Topological sorting is graph traversal with indegrees or DFS states</li><li>Union-Find is another connectivity tool when edges arrive/merge dynamically</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write NODES=?, EDGES=?, VISITED MEANS=? before code</li><li>If every edge has same cost and you need shortest steps, think BFS</li><li>For grids, write directions=[(1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)] once</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>Visited means the node has already been discovered under the search semantics and should not be re-added/reprocessed unnecessarily.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 503}, "p11-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Graph Traversal\n\nCORE → Translate the story into nodes, edges, and visited state. Choose DFS for reachability/components and BFS when shortest unweighted distance/layers matter.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Connectivity, components, islands\n- Grid cells with neighbor movement\n- Shortest number of steps in unweighted graph\n\nINVARIANT\nVisited means the node has already been discovered under the search semantics and should not be re-added/reprocessed unnecessarily.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nWith adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nGRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count islands in a grid.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Find shortest number of moves in an unweighted maze.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Find cheapest weighted route.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Detect whether course prerequisites contain a cycle.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: With adjacency lists, BFS/DFS visits each vertex once and examines each edge a constant number of times: O(V+E). Space is O(V) for visited plus queue/stack. Grid of R×C cells becomes O(RC).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">from collections import deque\nq = deque([start])\nseen = {start}\nwhile q:\n    node = q.popleft()\n    for nei in graph[node]:\n        if nei not in seen:\n            seen.add(nei)\n            q.append(nei)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Graph Traversal come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A -- B -- D\n|    |\nC -- E\n\nBFS from A: A | B C | D E\nDFS might: A B D E C</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GRID → GRAPH\n(r,c) node\n  ↑\n← + →  edges to valid neighbors\n  ↓\nvisited prevents loops/repeated work</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GRAPH TRAVERSAL = identify nodes/edges, define visited, then explore each reachable state once.</p></section>", "wordCount": 523}, "p12-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Find minimum travel time with positive edge weights.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Count connected components.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>Advanced graph problems ask more than reachability. Edge weights, cheapest connecting networks, dependency order, or “use every edge exactly once” change which algorithm is correct.</p><p>A road map with travel times is not the same as a map where every road counts as one step. BFS counts edges; Dijkstra chooses the cheapest known frontier. MST asks a different question: connect all cities as cheaply as possible, not travel from A to B.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Classify the graph objective before coding: shortest path, minimum spanning tree, topological order, or Eulerian traversal.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Network Delay Time: nodes are computers, directed edges carry travel time. BFS cannot just count hops because a 1-edge path may cost 100 while a 3-edge path costs 9. Dijkstra chooses smallest accumulated cost.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Enumerate all possible paths or all edge subsets. Path counts can explode exponentially.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Use problem structure: Dijkstra finalizes the cheapest frontier for non-negative weights; Prim/Kruskal greedily grows a minimum spanning tree; topological sort removes dependency-free nodes.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Dijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Advanced Graphs to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 580}, "p12-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Positive weighted shortest path → Dijkstra</li><li>Connect all nodes with minimum total edge cost → MST</li><li>Dependency order in DAG → topological sort</li><li>Use every directed edge/ticket exactly once → Eulerian/Hierholzer flavor</li><li>Unknown alphabet order inferred from word comparisons → graph + topo</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any graph with numbers</li><li>Any “minimum” problem</li><li>Any route problem without checking whether weights matter</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Unweighted shortest steps → BFS is simpler</li><li>Need only connectivity/components → plain DFS/BFS/Union-Find</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Dijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Use problem structure: Dijkstra finalizes the cheapest frontier for non-negative weights; Prim/Kruskal greedily grows a minimum spanning tree; topological sort removes dependency-free nodes.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Classify the graph objective before coding: shortest path, minimum spanning tree, topological order, or Eulerian traversal.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Number of Islands is a graph problem, but no weights/order/global optimization exist. Plain DFS/BFS is the correct simpler tool.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Positive weighted shortest path → Dijkstra, Connect all nodes with minimum total edge cost → MST?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The algorithm-specific frontier has a proof: Dijkstra pops the smallest currently reachable distance; MST adds the cheapest safe edge crossing the cut; topo processes nodes only when prerequisites are satisfied.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 563}, "p12-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Enumerate all possible paths or all edge subsets. Path counts can explode exponentially.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Use problem structure: Dijkstra finalizes the cheapest frontier for non-negative weights; Prim/Kruskal greedily grows a minimum spanning tree; topological sort removes dependency-free nodes.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The algorithm-specific frontier has a proof: Dijkstra pops the smallest currently reachable distance; MST adds the cheapest safe edge crossing the cut; topo processes nodes only when prerequisites are satisfied.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">dist = {start: 0}\nheap = [(0, start)]\nwhile heap:\n    d, node = heappop(heap)\n    if d != dist[node]: continue\n    for nei, w in graph[node]:\n        nd = d + w\n        if nd < dist.get(nei, INF):\n            dist[nei] = nd\n            heappush(heap, (nd, nei))</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Dijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>disconnected graph</li><li>negative weights</li><li>multiple edges</li><li>ties</li><li>stale heap entries</li><li>cycle in dependency graph</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Use Dijkstra with negative edges</li><li>Confuse shortest-path tree with minimum spanning tree</li><li>Forget stale distance checks</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 513}, "p12-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Network Delay Time: Dijkstra</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Cheapest Flights with K stops: shortest path + state/relaxation limit</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Min Cost Connect Points: MST</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Reconstruct Itinerary: Eulerian path + lexical ordering</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Alien Dictionary: infer edges + topological sort</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Swim in Rising Water: min-max path / priority frontier</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>Directed?</li><li>Are weights non-negative?</li><li>Need shortest path between two nodes or connect all nodes?</li><li>Can edges be reused?</li><li>Is ordering required to use all edges?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Use Dijkstra with negative edges</li><li>Confuse shortest-path tree with minimum spanning tree</li><li>Forget stale distance checks</li><li>Build topo edges incorrectly from invalid prefix ordering</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Classify the graph objective before coding: shortest path, minimum spanning tree, topological order, or Eulerian traversal.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Enumerate all possible paths or all edge subsets. Path counts can explode exponentially. The useful idea is: Use problem structure: Dijkstra finalizes the cheapest frontier for non-negative weights; Prim/Kruskal greedily grows a minimum spanning tree; topological sort removes dependency-free nodes.</p></section>", "wordCount": 547}, "p12-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>BFS</b></td><td>all edge costs equal / unweighted shortest path</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greedy + Heap</b></td><td>advanced graph algorithms often use both, but graph invariant determines correctness</td></tr><tr><td><b>DP</b></td><td>bounded-stop paths can sometimes be expressed as Bellman-Ford/DP over number of edges</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>Number of Islands is a graph problem, but no weights/order/global optimization exist. Plain DFS/BFS is the correct simpler tool.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Network Delay Time: Dijkstra</li><li>Cheapest Flights with K stops: shortest path + state/relaxation limit</li><li>Min Cost Connect Points: MST</li><li>Reconstruct Itinerary: Eulerian path + lexical ordering</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Positive weighted shortest path → Dijkstra</li><li>Connect all nodes with minimum total edge cost → MST</li><li>Dependency order in DAG → topological sort</li><li>Use every directed edge/ticket exactly once → Eulerian/Hierholzer flavor</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any graph with numbers</li><li>Any “minimum” problem</li><li>Any route problem without checking whether weights matter</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Number of Islands is a graph problem, but no weights/order/global optimization exist. Plain DFS/BFS is the correct simpler tool.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Find minimum travel time with positive edge weights.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Connect all points with minimum total connection cost.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Count connected components.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Order letters from lexicographically sorted alien words.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 505}, "p12-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard graph problems combine graph modeling with an extra state dimension. Do not memorize algorithm names first; identify what makes one state “better” and what can be safely finalized.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>State can be (node, stops) rather than node alone</li><li>MST cut property is the proof behind safe cheapest-edge choices</li><li>Hierholzer builds Eulerian routes by consuming edges then adding nodes on unwind</li><li>Alien Dictionary requires detecting invalid prefix cases</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Before algorithm name, write OBJECTIVE in one sentence</li><li>If weights appear, ask whether they affect optimality or are decorative</li><li>For shortest path, define what dist[state] means exactly</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The algorithm-specific frontier has a proof: Dijkstra pops the smallest currently reachable distance; MST adds the cheapest safe edge crossing the cut; topo processes nodes only when prerequisites are satisfied.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Dijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 522}, "p12-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Advanced Graphs\n\nCORE → Classify the graph objective before coding: shortest path, minimum spanning tree, topological order, or Eulerian traversal.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Positive weighted shortest path → Dijkstra\n- Connect all nodes with minimum total edge cost → MST\n- Dependency order in DAG → topological sort\n\nINVARIANT\nThe algorithm-specific frontier has a proof: Dijkstra pops the smallest currently reachable distance; MST adds the cheapest safe edge crossing the cut; topo processes nodes only when prerequisites are satisfied.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nDijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Find minimum travel time with positive edge weights.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Connect all points with minimum total connection cost.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Count connected components.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Order letters from lexicographically sorted alien words.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Dijkstra with a binary heap is typically O((V+E) log V). Prim is similar with adjacency lists + heap. Kruskal is dominated by sorting edges: O(E log E). Topological sort is O(V+E).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">dist = {start: 0}\nheap = [(0, start)]\nwhile heap:\n    d, node = heappop(heap)\n    if d != dist[node]: continue\n    for nei, w in graph[node]:\n        nd = d + w\n        if nd < dist.get(nei, INF):\n            dist[nei] = nd\n            heappush(heap, (nd, nei))</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Advanced Graphs come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">A --1--> B --2--> D\n \\10            ↑\n  -----> C --1-/\n\nBFS hop count may prefer A→C\nDijkstra compares accumulated COST</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">FIRST classify objective:\nshortest weighted path → DIJKSTRA\nconnect all cheapest      → MST\ndependency ordering       → TOPO\nuse every edge once       → EULERIAN</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> ADVANCED GRAPH = classify the objective first; the graph alone does not tell you the algorithm.</p></section>", "wordCount": 552}, "p13-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count ways to climb n stairs using 1 or 2 steps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Generate every subset of numbers.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>Dynamic Programming (DP) means a problem contains smaller repeated subproblems. Instead of solving the same smaller question again and again, save its answer. 1-D DP usually means one main index/amount/state changes.</p><p>Imagine climbing stairs. “Ways to reach step 10” depends on answers you already computed for steps 9 and 8. There is no reason to recursively recompute the same step thousands of times.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Define a state in plain English, write how it depends on smaller states, set base cases, then choose memoization or bottom-up iteration.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>House Robber: dp[i] = best money using houses up to i. Choice 1 skip house i → dp[i-1]. Choice 2 rob it → value[i] + dp[i-2]. Take max. This converts exponential take/skip recursion into O(n).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Recursive try-all branches repeatedly solve the same suffix/index/remaining amount. Fibonacci-style recursion is the classic exponential explosion.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Count unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain 1-D Dynamic Programming to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section>", "wordCount": 580}, "p13-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Best/count answer over prefixes or positions</li><li>Take/skip decisions</li><li>Same subproblem repeats in recursion</li><li>Future depends on a small number of previous states</li><li>Amount/index is the main changing dimension</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any optimization problem</li><li>Any recursion</li><li>Any array with “maximum”</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>One safe local choice collapses history → Greedy may be enough</li><li>Need enumerate actual combinations/paths → Backtracking</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Count unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Define a state in plain English, write how it depends on smaller states, set base cases, then choose memoization or bottom-up iteration.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Jump Game can be written as DP, but a greedy farthest-reach invariant collapses the state to O(1). The existence of a DP solution does not mean DP is best.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Best/count answer over prefixes or positions, Take/skip decisions?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “dp[i] (or memo state) always has one exact meaning, and every transition uses only smaller/already-solved states consistent with that meaning.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section>", "wordCount": 583}, "p13-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Recursive try-all branches repeatedly solve the same suffix/index/remaining amount. Fibonacci-style recursion is the classic exponential explosion.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>dp[i] (or memo state) always has one exact meaning, and every transition uses only smaller/already-solved states consistent with that meaning.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\"># example shape\ndp = [0] * (n + 1)\ndp[0] = BASE\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    dp[i] = combine(dp[i-1], dp[i-2], ...)\nreturn dp[n]</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Count unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>n=0/1</li><li>impossible state</li><li>infinity sentinel</li><li>negative values</li><li>circular adjacency</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Start coding before defining state</li><li>Wrong base cases</li><li>Use memo key missing part of state</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Recursive try-all branches repeatedly solve the same suffix/index/remaining amount. Fibonacci-style recursion is the classic exponential explosion. The useful idea is: Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p></section>", "wordCount": 547}, "p13-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Climbing Stairs: count ways</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>House Robber: take/skip max</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Decode Ways: count valid parses</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Word Break: prefix feasibility</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Coin Change: min coins for amount</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>LIS: best sequence ending/starting at index</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>What exactly should dp[i] mean?</li><li>What smaller states determine it?</li><li>What are base cases?</li><li>In what order must states be computed?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Start coding before defining state</li><li>Wrong base cases</li><li>Use memo key missing part of state</li><li>Iterate in an order that reads not-yet-computed states</li><li>Compress space before correctness is clear</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Define a state in plain English, write how it depends on smaller states, set base cases, then choose memoization or bottom-up iteration.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Recursive try-all branches repeatedly solve the same suffix/index/remaining amount. Fibonacci-style recursion is the classic exponential explosion. The useful idea is: Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p></section>", "wordCount": 532}, "p13-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Greedy</b></td><td>local choice has a proof and no history/state table is needed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Backtracking</b></td><td>need enumerate solutions rather than best/count with repeated states</td></tr><tr><td><b>2-D DP</b></td><td>state needs two independent indices/dimensions</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>Jump Game can be written as DP, but a greedy farthest-reach invariant collapses the state to O(1). The existence of a DP solution does not mean DP is best.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Climbing Stairs: count ways</li><li>House Robber: take/skip max</li><li>Decode Ways: count valid parses</li><li>Word Break: prefix feasibility</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Best/count answer over prefixes or positions</li><li>Take/skip decisions</li><li>Same subproblem repeats in recursion</li><li>Future depends on a small number of previous states</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any optimization problem</li><li>Any recursion</li><li>Any array with “maximum”</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Jump Game can be written as DP, but a greedy farthest-reach invariant collapses the state to O(1). The existence of a DP solution does not mean DP is best.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count ways to climb n stairs using 1 or 2 steps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Maximize money with no adjacent houses.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Generate every subset of numbers.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find minimum coins needed to make an amount.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Recursive try-all branches repeatedly solve the same suffix/index/remaining amount. Fibonacci-style recursion is the classic exponential explosion. The useful idea is: Name the repeated subproblem and cache it. Or fill answers from smallest state upward so every required dependency is already known.</p></section>", "wordCount": 545}, "p13-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard DP is state design. Ask what minimum information about the past makes the future independent. Too little state is incorrect; too much state explodes complexity.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Top-down memoization is often easier to derive; bottom-up can be faster/iterative</li><li>Many DPs are hidden DAG shortest/longest paths over states</li><li>Space compression is safe only if future states need a limited history</li><li>LIS can later be optimized beyond O(n²) using binary-search tails</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Write: STATE, RECURRENCE, BASE, ORDER before code</li><li>If recursion arguments repeat, highlight them—that tuple is likely the memo key</li><li>Use a tiny input and fill the dp table by hand</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>dp[i] (or memo state) always has one exact meaning, and every transition uses only smaller/already-solved states consistent with that meaning.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Count unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section>", "wordCount": 516}, "p13-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: 1-D Dynamic Programming\n\nCORE → Define a state in plain English, write how it depends on smaller states, set base cases, then choose memoization or bottom-up iteration.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Best/count answer over prefixes or positions\n- Take/skip decisions\n- Same subproblem repeats in recursion\n\nINVARIANT\ndp[i] (or memo state) always has one exact meaning, and every transition uses only smaller/already-solved states consistent with that meaning.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nCount unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).\n\nMEMORY HOOK\n1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Count ways to climb n stairs using 1 or 2 steps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Maximize money with no adjacent houses.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Generate every subset of numbers.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find minimum coins needed to make an amount.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Count unique states × work per state. If there are n indices and each state checks O(1) transitions, O(n). Coin Change may have amount×coins. Space is number of stored states; many 1-D recurrences compress to O(1).</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\"># example shape\ndp = [0] * (n + 1)\ndp[0] = BASE\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    dp[i] = combine(dp[i-1], dp[i-2], ...)\nreturn dp[n]</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should 1-D Dynamic Programming come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">state i depends on smaller states\n\ndp[i-2] ─┐\n          ├→ dp[i]\ndp[i-1] ─┘\n\ncompute left → right once</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">RECURSION TREE\n        f(5)\n      /      \\\n    f(4)     f(3)\n   /  \\      / \\\n f(3) f(2)  f(2) f(1)\n ^ repeated states → CACHE THEM</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 1-D DP = define one reusable subproblem, store it, and build bigger answers from smaller ones.</p></section>", "wordCount": 545}, "p14-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest common subsequence of two strings.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Count connected land components in a grid.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>2-D DP means the subproblem needs two changing coordinates, indices, or pieces of state—such as positions in two strings, row/column in a grid, or two interval boundaries.</p><p>Imagine comparing two words letter by letter. Knowing only your position in word A is not enough; you must also know where you are in word B. That pair (i,j) becomes the state.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Name both dimensions in plain English. Build transitions from smaller valid states and choose an iteration order that computes prerequisites first.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Longest Common Subsequence: dp[i][j] = LCS length of first i chars of A and first j chars of B. If last chars match, 1+dp[i-1][j-1]; otherwise max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]).</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Try all alignments/choices recursively. The same (i,j) pair is revisited many times, creating exponential recursion.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Cache every unique pair state or fill a table where each cell means one well-defined subproblem. Use arrows to show which earlier cells feed the current one.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Count states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain 2-D Dynamic Programming to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 597}, "p14-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Two strings/sequences interact</li><li>Grid paths/costs</li><li>State naturally looks like (i,j)</li><li>Edit/align/interleave two inputs</li><li>Interval DP uses (left,right)</li><li>Memo needs a tuple rather than one index</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Any matrix input</li><li>Any nested loop</li><li>Any problem with two arrays if one can be hashed/scanned directly</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Simple grid reachability → BFS/DFS may be enough</li><li>One index/amount fully describes subproblem → 1-D DP</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Count states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Cache every unique pair state or fill a table where each cell means one well-defined subproblem. Use arrows to show which earlier cells feed the current one.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Name both dimensions in plain English. Build transitions from smaller valid states and choose an iteration order that computes prerequisites first.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Number of Islands uses a 2-D grid but is not naturally a DP table; it is graph connectivity, so DFS/BFS is simpler.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Two strings/sequences interact, Grid paths/costs?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “Every dp[i][j] cell has exactly one semantic meaning, and the traversal order guarantees every predecessor needed by its recurrence is already correct.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Classification protocol:</b> before code, say the strongest structural clue, the target complexity, and one reason a nearby pattern loses. If wrong, note the missed clue in one sentence.</p><p>Redraw the anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 599}, "p14-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Try all alignments/choices recursively. The same (i,j) pair is revisited many times, creating exponential recursion.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Cache every unique pair state or fill a table where each cell means one well-defined subproblem. Use arrows to show which earlier cells feed the current one.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>Every dp[i][j] cell has exactly one semantic meaning, and the traversal order guarantees every predecessor needed by its recurrence is already correct.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\ninitialize_base_rows_and_columns(dp)\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[i][j] = transition(dp, i, j)\nreturn dp[n][m]</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Count states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>empty string</li><li>zero-length grid</li><li>impossible alignment</li><li>first row/column initialization</li><li>interval length 1</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Rows/columns have no semantic definition</li><li>Wrong loop order</li><li>Base initialization off by one</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 522}, "p14-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Unique Paths: grid coordinates</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>LCS/Edit Distance: two string indices</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Interleaving String: positions in two sources</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Distinct Subsequences: source index + target index</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Burst Balloons: interval boundaries</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Regex: string index + pattern index</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>What does i mean? What does j mean?</li><li>Are prefixes, suffixes, or intervals easier?</li><li>Which neighboring states transition into this one?</li><li>What are base row/column meanings?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Rows/columns have no semantic definition</li><li>Wrong loop order</li><li>Base initialization off by one</li><li>Confuse substring (contiguous) with subsequence</li><li>Forget an extra boolean/state dimension such as holding/cooldown</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Name both dimensions in plain English. Build transitions from smaller valid states and choose an iteration order that computes prerequisites first.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 515}, "p14-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1-D DP</b></td><td>only one changing dimension is needed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Graph traversal</b></td><td>state graph exists but reachability/shortest edges are simpler than table recurrence</td></tr><tr><td><b>Backtracking</b></td><td>need actual enumerated choices, not aggregate best/count</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>Number of Islands uses a 2-D grid but is not naturally a DP table; it is graph connectivity, so DFS/BFS is simpler.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Unique Paths: grid coordinates</li><li>LCS/Edit Distance: two string indices</li><li>Interleaving String: positions in two sources</li><li>Distinct Subsequences: source index + target index</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Two strings/sequences interact</li><li>Grid paths/costs</li><li>State naturally looks like (i,j)</li><li>Edit/align/interleave two inputs</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Any matrix input</li><li>Any nested loop</li><li>Any problem with two arrays if one can be hashed/scanned directly</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>Number of Islands uses a 2-D grid but is not naturally a DP table; it is graph connectivity, so DFS/BFS is simpler.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest common subsequence of two strings.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Count paths in a grid moving right/down.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Count connected land components in a grid.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Minimum edit operations to transform one string into another.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Try all alignments/choices recursively. The same (i,j) pair is revisited many times, creating exponential recursion. The useful idea is: Cache every unique pair state or fill a table where each cell means one well-defined subproblem. Use arrows to show which earlier cells feed the current one.</p></section>", "wordCount": 556}, "p14-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard 2-D DP is mostly about discovering the dimensions and legal transitions. Once the state sentence and dependency arrows are correct, code becomes table bookkeeping.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Memoized DFS is often clearer for irregular DAG-like grids</li><li>Interval DP usually grows by interval length</li><li>Regex * means zero or more and requires careful state transitions</li><li>Space compression depends on which previous row/cells remain needed</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Label axes with meanings before filling a table</li><li>Draw arrows into one cell to derive recurrence</li><li>Start with full O(nm) table; compress only after passing tests</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>Every dp[i][j] cell has exactly one semantic meaning, and the traversal order guarantees every predecessor needed by its recurrence is already correct.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Count states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 519}, "p14-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: 2-D Dynamic Programming\n\nCORE → Name both dimensions in plain English. Build transitions from smaller valid states and choose an iteration order that computes prerequisites first.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Two strings/sequences interact\n- Grid paths/costs\n- State naturally looks like (i,j)\n\nINVARIANT\nEvery dp[i][j] cell has exactly one semantic meaning, and the traversal order guarantees every predecessor needed by its recurrence is already correct.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nCount states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\n2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Longest common subsequence of two strings.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Count paths in a grid moving right/down.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Count connected land components in a grid.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Minimum edit operations to transform one string into another.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Count states: n×m cells. If each cell does O(1) work, O(nm) time and O(nm) space. If each state loops over k choices, O(nmk). Rows can sometimes be compressed to O(m) space.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\ninitialize_base_rows_and_columns(dp)\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp[i][j] = transition(dp, i, j)\nreturn dp[n][m]</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should 2-D Dynamic Programming come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">        j → B chars\n      0  1  2  3\ni 0 [0][0][0][0]\n↓ 1 [0][ ][ ][ ]\n  2 [0][ ][X][ ]\n\nX may depend on ↑, ←, and ↖</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">STATE = (i, j)\nmeaning: “answer for prefix A[:i] and B[:j]”\n\nIf you cannot finish that sentence, the DP is not ready to code.</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> 2-D DP = two coordinates fully describe a reusable subproblem; each table cell has one sentence meaning.</p></section>", "wordCount": 558}, "p15-d1": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 1 goal:</b> Build the data-structure/algorithm idea from scratch. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>0. Cold guess — do this before reading</h3><p>Read these tiny prompts. Guess the pattern in 20 seconds each. Being wrong is useful because your brain now has a prediction to correct.</p><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Determine whether the end of an array is reachable by jumps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>0/1 knapsack maximizing value under capacity.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What is this, in plain English?</h3><p>A greedy algorithm makes a local choice and never goes back. That only works when you can prove the choice is safe—meaning some optimal solution can include it or your maintained frontier dominates all alternatives.</p><p>If you want to attend the maximum number of non-overlapping meetings, choosing the meeting that finishes earliest leaves the most room for everything later. That local choice has a reason, not just a guess.</p><p><b>The real job:</b> Compress many possible histories into one best frontier/summary and prove discarded histories can never beat it later.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. First mental picture</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><p>Trace the arrows/boxes with your finger. The picture matters more than syntax today.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. One tiny worked example</h3><p>Jump Game: keep farthest index reachable so far. At index i, if i > farthest, failure. Otherwise update farthest=max(farthest, i+nums[i]). You do not need to remember every path—only the best frontier.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. The slow idea vs the useful idea</h3><p><b>Brute force:</b> Try every combination/order of choices to find the global optimum—often exponential.</p><p><b>Bridge:</b> Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Beginner vocabulary</h3><div class=\"lesson-chip-row \"><span class=\"lesson-chip\">state = facts carried forward</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">invariant = what must stay true</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">candidate = a possible answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">constraint = limit on input/answer</span><span class=\"lesson-chip\">auxiliary space = extra memory you create</span></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity intuition</h3><p>Many greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. 60-second teach-back</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Explain Greedy Decision-Making to a 12-year-old.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What repeated work does this pattern remove?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does its main state/structure remember?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson before solving.</b> For each problem, spend 60–90 seconds naming the data shape, brute force, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Save only the clue you missed—not a copied solution.</p><p>Redraw these two anchors once:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section>", "wordCount": 597}, "p15-d2": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 2 goal:</b> Train the classifier before touching code. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Pattern fingerprint: strong clues</h3><p>Strong clues describe structure. They are much safer than memorizing random keywords.</p><ul class=\"strong-clues\"><li>Track farthest reachable position</li><li>Intervals can be sorted and selected safely</li><li>Best ending-here/current balance/frontier is enough</li><li>A local choice dominates alternatives by an exchange argument</li><li>DP state seems to collapse to one best summary</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Weak clues — do NOT overreact</h3><p>These may appear, but they are not enough by themselves:</p><ul class=\"weak-clues\"><li>Problem asks for min/max</li><li>A locally biggest number looks attractive</li><li>Sorting appears</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Anti-clues: when your first guess is probably wrong</h3><ul class=\"danger-clues\"><li>Choice safety cannot be proved and future depends on multiple histories → DP</li><li>Need explore alternatives/undo → Backtracking</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Constraints are clues too</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">ROUGH INTERVIEW HEURISTIC (not a law)\n\nn ≈ 20        exponential may be possible\nn ≈ 100       O(n³) sometimes possible\nn ≈ 1,000     O(n²) may be possible\nn ≈ 100,000   usually O(n log n) / O(n)\nn ≈ 1,000,000 usually near-linear\n\nAlways consider language, constants and actual time limits.</pre><p>For this pattern: Many greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Question surgery</h3><p>Take a prompt and underline only the information that changes the algorithm: input shape, ordering, whether choices must be contiguous, what must be optimized, and the largest possible <code>n</code>. Then ask: <b>what repeated work would brute force do?</b></p><p>Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Strip away the story</h3><p>Interviewers may talk about users, boats, servers, words, meetings, roads, or prices. Replace nouns with structure. Your compressed sentence for this week should be: <b>Compress many possible histories into one best frontier/summary and prove discarded histories can never beat it later.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Counterexample: looks similar, but is not this pattern</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>0/1 Knapsack often tempts “best value/weight ratio,” but that local choice can block a better combination. DP is needed because multiple histories remain relevant.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Decision gate</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Does the input/story contain one of these strong structural signals: Track farthest reachable position, Intervals can be sorted and selected safely?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain the state/invariant in one sentence: “The maintained frontier/summary is at least as good as every discarded history with respect to all future decisions.”?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I rule out at least one nearby pattern for a structural reason, not just a keyword?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Do the constraints make the brute-force approach too expensive?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Close the lesson.</b> Spend 60–90 seconds on each problem naming the data shape, target complexity, strongest clue, likely pattern, and one competing pattern. Record only the clue or invariant you missed.</p><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section>", "wordCount": 556}, "p15-d3": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 3 goal:</b> Understand why it works and how to count the cost. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Start from brute force, not the template</h3><p>Try every combination/order of choices to find the global optimum—often exponential.</p><p>Now ask which work is repeated and what information lets you skip it. Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. The invariant — your correctness anchor</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>The maintained frontier/summary is at least as good as every discarded history with respect to all future decisions.</p></div><p>If you cannot explain why every update preserves this sentence, pause. Coding before the invariant is clear is how off-by-one and “works on examples only” bugs happen.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Skeleton, not a memorized LeetCode solution</h3><pre class=\"lesson-code\">best = initial_state\nfor x in ordered_or_streaming_input:\n    best = safe_local_update(best, x)\n    if impossible(best):\n        return failure\nreturn derive_answer(best)</pre><p>Rewrite this from memory once. Rename variables if you want. What matters is the order of state transitions and the invariant.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Complexity: derive it from operations</h3><p>Many greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.</p><p><b>Rule:</b> count how many unique states/items exist, then how many times each can be processed. Sequential phases add; nested independent work multiplies; a heap operation adds a <code>log</code>; recursion requires counting states/branches.</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">COUNT, DON'T GUESS\nitems/states × work per item/state = time\nmaximum extra live state              = space</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Edge cases worth testing before submit</h3><ul><li>all negative</li><li>unreachable gap</li><li>ties</li><li>empty list</li><li>reset points</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Spot-the-bug mindset</h3><p>Do not just ask “does my code run?” Ask which invariant could be broken by equality, empty input, duplicate values, pointer crossing, stale state, or update order.</p><ul><li>Say “greedy” without proof</li><li>Choose largest/smallest locally because it feels intuitive</li><li>Ignore a counterexample where future constraints matter</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Blank-editor recall</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Write the invariant without scrolling up.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Write the skeleton from memory.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Explain the time complexity in sentences, not Big-O symbols only.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Blank-editor rule:</b> state the invariant, write the generic skeleton, and derive complexity from operations before reading any solution. If stuck, record the exact transition you could not justify.</p><p>Redraw these anchors from memory:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Try every combination/order of choices to find the global optimum—often exponential. The useful idea is: Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p></section>", "wordCount": 552}, "p15-d4": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 4 goal:</b> Strip away story noise and expose the underlying structure. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. How interviewers disguise a pattern</h3><p>They usually do not say the algorithm name. They change the nouns, add irrelevant business rules, hide ordering inside a constraint, or ask an optimization instead of a direct search. Your job is to remove the story and preserve only structure.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Mutation ladder — same DNA, harder clothes</h3><ol class=\"mutation-ladder\"><li><span>Level 1</span><p>Maximum Subarray: best ending here (Kadane)</p></li><li><span>Level 2</span><p>Jump Game: farthest reachable</p></li><li><span>Level 3</span><p>Jump Game II: current layer/farthest next layer</p></li><li><span>Level 4</span><p>Gas Station: reset impossible starts</p></li><li><span>Level 5</span><p>Partition Labels: cut at maximum last occurrence</p></li><li><span>Level 6</span><p>Interval scheduling: earliest finishing safe choice</p></li></ol></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>3. Question surgery checklist</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>What is the raw data shape?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>What relationship must be found or optimized?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What does brute force repeatedly reconsider?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What fact lets us safely skip work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>5</span><p>What is the nearest competing pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. A second mental map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Clarifying questions that can reveal the pattern</h3><ul><li>What local state summarizes all useful history?</li><li>Why is discarded history dominated?</li><li>Does sorting create a safe choice order?</li><li>Can a counterexample break the proposed local rule?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Classic traps interviewers expect</h3><ul><li>Say “greedy” without proof</li><li>Choose largest/smallest locally because it feels intuitive</li><li>Ignore a counterexample where future constraints matter</li><li>Use wrong interval sorting key</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Your “story → structure” sentence</h3><p>Practice saying: “Ignore the story. This is really <b>Compress many possible histories into one best frontier/summary and prove discarded histories can never beat it later.</b>.” If you can compress a long prompt into that sentence, recognition gets dramatically faster.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Story-stripping protocol:</b> replace nouns with data structure, relationship, constraints, and objective. Then say, “This is really ___ because ___.” On the two problems, write the disguised clue you nearly ignored and the clarification question that would expose it.</p><p>Do not save whole solutions. Save a compact note: “I saw ___, but the decisive clue was ___, therefore ___.” That is the memory route you want during an interview.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Try every combination/order of choices to find the global optimum—often exponential. The useful idea is: Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p></section>", "wordCount": 549}, "p15-d5": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 5 goal:</b> Choose this pattern over its nearest competitors. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. Why pattern discrimination matters</h3><p>If every exercise in a week is labeled with the answer, your brain learns the label, not the decision. Today you must decide why this pattern wins over nearby options.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Nearest competitors</h3><table class=\"lesson-table\"><thead><tr><th>Nearby pattern</th><th>Prefer it when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>DP</b></td><td>multiple non-dominated histories must be remembered</td></tr><tr><td><b>Heap</b></td><td>greedy choice exists but candidates arrive/change dynamically</td></tr><tr><td><b>Backtracking</b></td><td>must test alternatives because no local proof</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. “Why NOT?” drill</h3><p>For every problem, name one plausible wrong pattern and reject it using structure. Example: <b>0/1 Knapsack often tempts “best value/weight ratio,” but that local choice can block a better combination. DP is needed because multiple histories remain relevant.</b></p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Hybrid solutions are normal</h3><p>Real interview questions often combine patterns. Sorting may enable Two Pointers; a Hash Map may support a Linked List; Binary Search may call a Sliding Window feasibility check; Graph search may use a Heap. Pick the <b>dominant invariant</b> first, then name support structures.</p><ul><li>Maximum Subarray: best ending here (Kadane)</li><li>Jump Game: farthest reachable</li><li>Jump Game II: current layer/farthest next layer</li><li>Gas Station: reset impossible starts</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Strong vs weak evidence recap</h3><div class=\"two-col\"><div><h4>Strong</h4><ul><li>Track farthest reachable position</li><li>Intervals can be sorted and selected safely</li><li>Best ending-here/current balance/frontier is enough</li><li>A local choice dominates alternatives by an exchange argument</li></ul></div><div><h4>Weak</h4><ul><li>Problem asks for min/max</li><li>A locally biggest number looks attractive</li><li>Sorting appears</li></ul></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Counterexample test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout warning\"><p>0/1 Knapsack often tempts “best value/weight ratio,” but that local choice can block a better combination. DP is needed because multiple histories remain relevant.</p></div><p>If one counterexample breaks your decision rule, improve the rule instead of memorizing an exception.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>7. Mixed classifier</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Determine whether the end of an array is reachable by jumps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Choose maximum number of non-overlapping intervals.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>0/1 knapsack maximizing value under capacity.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find a valid gas-station start around a circle.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Mixed-pattern protocol:</b> force yourself to name two candidate patterns before choosing one. Your answer is incomplete until you explain why the runner-up fails. If a problem is hybrid, name the dominant invariant first and supporting structure second.</p><p>After each solve, save one contrast note such as “Sliding Window because the whole contiguous range has state; not Two Pointers because endpoints are not the candidates.” These contrast sentences create fast discrimination.</p><p>Redraw both anchors:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Memory cement</h3><p><b>Memory cement:</b> Connect the pattern to the waste it removes. Try every combination/order of choices to find the global optimum—often exponential. The useful idea is: Find a dominance rule: if state A is at least as good as state B for every possible future, keep A and discard B. That collapse is the greedy insight.</p></section>", "wordCount": 547}, "p15-d6": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 6 goal:</b> Learn the observations that make hard variants collapse. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. What makes hard questions hard?</h3><p>Hard greedy problems are proof problems. The code may be five lines; finding the domination/exchange argument is the entire interview. Train yourself to search for what can be safely forgotten.</p><p>The hard part is usually the observation or representation, not typing the final loop.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>2. Advanced observations worth owning</h3><ul><li>Exchange arguments prove a greedy choice can be inserted into an optimal solution</li><li>Frontier proofs show one state dominates others</li><li>Jump Game II resembles BFS layers without building a queue</li><li>Gas Station uses total feasibility plus local reset logic</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Smart interview hacks</h3><ul><li>Try to invent a counterexample to your greedy choice before coding</li><li>Write what your single “best so far” variable means</li><li>If two histories are incomparable for future, greedy compression is probably invalid</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Proof before code</h3><p>Return to the invariant: <b>The maintained frontier/summary is at least as good as every discarded history with respect to all future decisions.</b> For a hard variant, ask what extra state or preprocessing is needed so this invariant becomes true again.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Hard-problem anatomy</h3><p>When stuck, do not stare at code. Write five lines:</p><ul><li>Brute force candidate space = ?</li><li>Repeated/wasted work = ?</li><li>What information would let me discard/compress/prune/finalize?</li><li>Which invariant would make that safe?</li><li>What complexity must constraints allow?</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>6. Complexity nuance</h3><p>Many greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.</p><p>Hard variants often add a preprocessing step, heap, sort, or second state dimension. State total complexity as a sum of phases and keep the dominant term.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Follow-up mutation drill</h3><p>Take one of today’s scheduled problems. Change one assumption: allow duplicates, remove sorting, require all answers, require streaming input, or add weights. Ask whether the same pattern survives. This is how you prepare for interviewer follow-ups.</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>8. Five-minute confidence check</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Can I derive the invariant from scratch?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Can I explain a hard variant without solution code?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>Can I name one mutation that breaks this pattern?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Hard-problem protocol:</b> give yourself permission to stop coding and hunt for the missing observation. Write brute-force candidate space, wasted work, the state you wish you could know instantly, and what proof would let you discard or compress candidates.</p><p>After each problem, record the breakthrough in one sentence rather than copying implementation. Then redraw:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section>", "wordCount": 523}, "p15-d7": {"html": "<p class=\"lesson-lead\"><b>Day 7 goal:</b> Compress the week into a fast interview memory map. You only need basic variables, loops, and <code>if/else</code>. Do not try to memorize the final code; build a picture you can reconstruct.</p>\n    <div class=\"lesson-memory-hook\"><span>ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOK</span><strong>GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</strong></div><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>1. One-screen memory map</h3><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">PATTERN: Greedy Decision-Making\n\nCORE → Compress many possible histories into one best frontier/summary and prove discarded histories can never beat it later.\n\nSTRONG SIGNALS\n- Track farthest reachable position\n- Intervals can be sorted and selected safely\n- Best ending-here/current balance/frontier is enough\n\nINVARIANT\nThe maintained frontier/summary is at least as good as every discarded history with respect to all future decisions.\n\nCOMPLEXITY\nMany greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.\n\nMEMORY HOOK\nGREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>2. Rapid classifier — no coding</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>Determine whether the end of an array is reachable by jumps.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Choose maximum number of non-overlapping intervals.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>0/1 knapsack maximizing value under capacity.</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>Find a valid gas-station start around a circle.</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>3. Complexity oral test</h3><p>Explain this without looking: Many greedy scans are O(n) time and O(1) space. If sorting creates the safe order, total time is O(n log n). A heap may make it O(n log n) or O(n log k) when the best eligible choice changes dynamically.</p><p>Then answer: what input size would make the brute-force approach suspicious? Which extra structure changes the space complexity?</p></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>4. Cold skeleton test</h3><p>Close this lesson mentally and write the generic skeleton from memory. Then compare:</p><pre class=\"lesson-code\">best = initial_state\nfor x in ordered_or_streaming_input:\n    best = safe_local_update(best, x)\n    if impossible(best):\n        return failure\nreturn derive_answer(best)</pre></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>5. Interview checklist</h3><ul><li>Restate the problem in structural terms.</li><li>Extract constraints and target complexity.</li><li>Describe brute force first.</li><li>Name candidate pattern + one competing pattern.</li><li>State the invariant/proof.</li><li>Walk one tiny example.</li><li>Code cleanly.</li><li>Test edge cases.</li><li>State time and space from operations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"lesson-section active\"><h3>6. Teach it back in 60 seconds</h3><div class=\"lesson-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>1</span><p>When should Greedy Decision-Making come to mind?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>2</span><p>Why does it work?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>3</span><p>What is the nearest pattern you might confuse it with?</p></div><div class=\"quiz-row\"><span>4</span><p>What hard variant taught you the most?</p></div></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>7. Week exit test</h3><div class=\"lesson-callout strong\"><p>You are “green” only if you can identify the pattern from an unlabeled prompt, justify it with structural clues, state the invariant, derive complexity, and reject a nearby pattern without looking at notes.</p></div></section><section class=\"lesson-section \"><h3>Before today’s two problems</h3><p><b>Certification protocol:</b> close all notes. Classify before coding, state one competing pattern, give the invariant, estimate complexity, and reconstruct the skeleton. Mark green only when those steps feel reproducible—not because you remember the specific LeetCode answer.</p><p>Final visual recall:</p><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">many possible paths\n path A ─┐\n path B ─┼─ compare → keep FARTHEST frontier only\n path C ─┘\n\nall dominated histories disappear</pre><pre class=\"lesson-diagram\">GREEDY PROOF QUESTION\nIf I make choice X now, can any optimal solution be transformed\nto make X without becoming worse?\n        yes → promising greedy rule</pre><p><b>Memory hook:</b> GREEDY = keep one provably dominant frontier and permanently discard weaker histories.</p></section>", "wordCount": 536}};

Object.assign(patternMeta, extraPatternMeta);
Object.assign(lessonDocs, extraLessonDocs);

const stageNames = [
  'Foundations: from zero to a mental picture',
  'Recognition: see the fingerprint in the wording',
  'Invariant, template & complexity',
  'Interviewer disguises & question surgery',
  'Pattern discrimination & combinations',
  'Advanced / hard-problem lens',
  'Mastery: instant recognition under pressure',
];

// Lessons are chosen by the day's position among the study days of its block, so a rest
// day inside a week does not shift the stages. Blocks shorter than 7 days list the stages
// they cover in extraLessonStages (or block.lessonStages).
export function lessonForDay(day, goal, resources={}) {
  if (!day || day.type !== 'study' || !day.patternNumber) return null;
  const pattern = goal.patterns.find(x=>x.number===day.patternNumber);
  const p = patternMeta[day.patternNumber];
  const block = goal.blocks?.find(b=>b.id===day.blockId);
  if(!pattern || !p || !block) return null;
  const studyDays = goal.schedule.filter(d=>d.blockId===block.id && d.type==='study');
  const position = Math.max(0, studyDays.findIndex(d=>d.id===day.id));
  const stages = block.lessonStages || extraLessonStages[day.patternNumber] || [0,1,2,3,4,5,6];
  const stageIndex = stages[Math.min(position, stages.length-1)];
  const id = `p${pattern.number}-d${Math.min(position, stages.length-1)+1}`;
  const doc = lessonDocs[id];
  const firstLinked = (day.resourceIds||[]).map(id=>resources[id]).find(r=>r?.patternNumber===pattern.number) || (day.resourceIds||[]).map(id=>resources[id]).find(Boolean);
  const links = [];
  // Several patterns originally pointed at a YouTube *search* page; prefer a real walkthrough.
  if (p.video && !p.video.includes('/results?')) links.push({label:p.videoLabel || 'Pattern video', url:p.video});
  if (firstLinked?.video && !links.some(l=>l.url===firstLinked.video)) links.push({label:`NeetCode video: ${firstLinked.title}`, url:firstLinked.video});
  links.push({label:'NeetCode list', url:nc(p.topic)});
  links.push({label:'LeetCode topic', url:p.leetcode});
  return {
    id, patternNumber:pattern.number, patternName:pattern.name, dayIndex:position+1,
    title:`Learn: ${pattern.name}`, subtitle:`Day ${position+1} · ${stageNames[stageIndex]}`,
    stage:stageNames[stageIndex], readMinutes:20, wordCount:doc?.wordCount || 0, links,
    html:doc?.html || '<p>Lesson content unavailable.</p>', detector:pattern.detector, exitTarget:pattern.exitTarget,
  };
}

export function lessonById(goal, lessonId, resources={}) {
  for (const day of goal.schedule || []) {
    const lesson = lessonForDay(day, goal, resources);
    if (lesson?.id === lessonId) return lesson;
  }
  return null;
}

export const lessonAudit = {
  count: Object.keys(lessonDocs).length,
  minWords: Math.min(...Object.values(lessonDocs).map(x=>x.wordCount)),
  maxWords: Math.max(...Object.values(lessonDocs).map(x=>x.wordCount)),
};
